Random samples are centred on the true value; larger random samples are simply more precise. Voluntary samples miss the true value, and the larger voluntary sample misses it just as much, only more confidently. #strong[A biased sample cannot be fixed by making it bigger.] The only remedies are a better sampling method, or knowing enough about the bias to correct for it.

=== Non-response and attrition
<non-response-and-attrition>
The same problem appears in the real data. Not every student answered the final open-ended question, and some left the study after the first year. The comparison below shows whether the students who remained are like those who did not:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("leavers ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("setdiff");#NormalTok("(students");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("student_id,");],
[#NormalTok("                   semesters");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("student_id[semesters");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("semester ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#NormalTok("students ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("stayed =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("if_else");#NormalTok("(student_id ");#SpecialCharTok("%in%");#NormalTok(" leavers, ");#StringTok("\"Left after year 1\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Stayed\"");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("group_by");#NormalTok("(stayed) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("summarise");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("students =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("n");#NormalTok("(),");],
[#NormalTok("            ");#AttributeTok("percent_considered_dropout =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("100");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("*");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok(")))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 2 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  stayed            students percent_considered_dropout");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>                <int>                      <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 Left after year 1       37                         59");],
[#NormalTok("2 Stayed                 563                         12");],));
]
]
Of the 37 students who left, 59% had considered dropping out, compared with 12% of those who stayed. Any analysis of semesters 3 and 4 is therefore based on a group that is less likely to be struggling than the students who started. Chapter 6 describes this missing data in detail, and Chapter 10 uses a model that makes the best use of the incomplete records.

=== Generalising from the sample
<generalising-from-the-sample>
The wellbeing study's sample is all graduate students at one university who agreed to take part. Its results apply most directly to that university, and to others like it, only by argument: similar students, similar programmes, similar pressures. A thesis should say this plainly in its limitations section. Claiming less than the data allows is rarely criticised; claiming more is.

== Planning the analysis before the data
<planning-the-analysis-before-the-data>
#emph[In one sentence: decide how you will judge your hypotheses before the data can influence you.]

=== An analysis plan
<an-analysis-plan>
For every hypothesis, the plan names the variables, the analysis, and what would count as support. Writing it before collecting data forces you to check that you will collect everything you need, in a form you can analyse:

#figure([
#table(
  columns: (20%, 20%, 20%, 20%, 20%),
  align: (auto,auto,auto,auto,auto,),
  table.header([Hypothesis], [Outcome], [Predictor(s)], [Analysis], [Support if],),
  table.hline(),
  [Students sleep less than 7 hours], [#NormalTok("sleep_hours"); (semester 1)], [none], [One-sample t-test against 7], [The 95% confidence interval lies below 7],
  [The workshop raises wellbeing], [#NormalTok("wellbeing"); (semester 2)], [#NormalTok("workshop");], [Two-sample t-test; mixed model over semesters], [Invited students higher; interval excludes 0],
  [Stress raises the odds of considering dropout], [#NormalTok("considering_dropout");], [stress score, with background variables], [Logistic regression], [Odds ratio above 1; interval excludes 1],
)
], caption: figure.caption(
position: top, 
[
Part of the study's analysis plan
]), 
