=== Bootstrap confidence intervals
<bootstrap-confidence-intervals>
The formula above works for averages. For other statistics, such as the median, there is no simple formula. The #strong[bootstrap] solves this with an idea of remarkable simplicity: treat the sample as if it were the population, and draw many new samples from it #emph[with replacement], so that each new sample contains some people twice and leaves others out. The spread of the statistic across these resamples estimates its sampling distribution.

Suppose the survey had reached only 40 students, and a confidence interval were needed for their median caffeine intake:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("small_sample ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sample");#NormalTok("(caffeine, ");#DecValTok("40");#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("median");#NormalTok("(small_sample)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 150");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("boot_medians ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("replicate");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2000");#NormalTok(", ");#FunctionTok("median");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("sample");#NormalTok("(small_sample, ");#AttributeTok("replace =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")))");],
[#FunctionTok("quantile");#NormalTok("(boot_medians, ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("0.025");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("0.975");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok(" 2.5% 97.5% ");],
[#NormalTok("110.0 177.5 ");],));
]
]
Sampling with replacement, the argument #NormalTok("replace = TRUE");, is what makes this a bootstrap. The middle 95% of the 2,000 bootstrap medians, from the 2.5th to the 97.5th percentile, forms the 95% bootstrap confidence interval. The median of all 600 students, 160 mg, lies inside it. The same few lines work for almost any statistic.

== The logic of hypothesis testing
<the-logic-of-hypothesis-testing>
A confidence interval describes which values are plausible. A #strong[hypothesis test] addresses a sharper question: whether the data is consistent with a particular claim. The claim that is tested is the #strong[null hypothesis], $H_0$, the statement that nothing is going on: no difference, no relationship. The researcher's own expectation is the #strong[alternative hypothesis], $H_1$. For the workshop, $H_0$ states that the workshop has no effect on wellbeing, and $H_1$ that it does.

Chapter 5 explained why tests are built around the null hypothesis: it is precise enough to calculate with. If the workshop had no effect, any difference between the invited and the not-invited students would be due only to which students happened to receive an invitation. The test asks whether the observed difference is larger than such chance differences usually are. That question can be answered directly, by simulation, before any formula is introduced.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
#strong[Question:] does the workshop improve wellbeing (RQ3)? It is a causal question, and the random invitation allows a causal answer. #strong[Unit of analysis:] the student, with one wellbeing score each, at the end of semester 2. #strong[Variables:] wellbeing (0 to 100, interval) is the outcome; the invitation (nominal, two groups) is the predictor. #strong[Evidence against $H_1$:] a difference near zero, or in favour of the students who were not invited.

]
, 
title: 
[
Think before you analyse
]
, 
background_color: 
rgb("#dae6fb")
, 
icon_color: 
rgb("#0758E5")
, 
icon: 
fa-info()
, 
body_background_color: 
white
)
]
=== A permutation test
<a-permutation-test>
The data needed is each student's workshop group together with their wellbeing at the end of semester 2:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("semester2 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" semesters ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(semester ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(students, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id))");],
[#FunctionTok("nrow");#NormalTok("(semester2)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 600");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("observed_gap ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("wellbeing[semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("workshop ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Invited\"");#NormalTok("]) ");#SpecialCharTok("-");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("wellbeing[semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("workshop ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Not invited\"");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("observed_gap");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 5.25");],));
]
]
Invited students scored 5.2 points higher. If the null hypothesis were true, the labels "Invited" and "Not invited" would carry no information about wellbeing: each student would have had the same score whichever label they had received. Under that assumption the labels can be shuffled at random among the students, and the difference recalculated. Each shuffle shows one difference that chance alone could have produced. Repeating the shuffle thousands of times builds the #strong[null distribution], the range of differences to expect when the workshop does nothing:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("shuffled_gaps ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("replicate");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("5000");#NormalTok(", {");],
[#NormalTok("  shuffled ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sample");#NormalTok("(semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("workshop)");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("wellbeing[shuffled ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Invited\"");#NormalTok("]) ");#SpecialCharTok("-");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("wellbeing[shuffled ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Not invited\"");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("})");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(shuffled_gaps)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("   Min. 1st Qu.  Median    Mean 3rd Qu.    Max. ");],
[#NormalTok("-3.6033 -0.6367  0.0100  0.0130  0.6633  3.2700 ");],));
]
]
Here #NormalTok("sample()"); without a size simply shuffles the whole column. #ref(<fig-permutation>, supplement: [Figure]) places the observed difference against the 5,000 shuffled ones.

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("gap =");#NormalTok(" shuffled_gaps), ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" gap)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_histogram");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("binwidth =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.25");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("fill =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"grey70\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"white\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_vline");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("xintercept =");#NormalTok(" observed_gap, ");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"#b2182b\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("linewidth =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Difference in average wellbeing (invited minus not invited)\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("       ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Number of shuffles\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("12");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("07-hypothesis-testing_files/figure-typst/fig-permutation-1.svg", alt: "A histogram of shuffled differences centred on zero, mostly between minus 3 and plus 3 points. A red vertical line marks the observed difference of about 5 points, far to the right of all the shuffled differences."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
The null distribution from 5,000 shuffles of the workshop labels, and the observed difference (red line). No shuffle came close to the observed difference.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-permutation>


Shuffled differences cluster around zero and rarely exceed 3 points in either direction. The observed difference lies far beyond all of them. The proportion of shuffles that produce a difference at least as large as the observed one, in either direction, is the #strong[p-value]:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("abs");#NormalTok("(shuffled_gaps) ");#SpecialCharTok(">=");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("abs");#NormalTok("(observed_gap))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0");],));
]
]
Not one of the 5,000 shuffles matched the observed difference. The p-value is not literally zero: a finite number of shuffles can only show that it is smaller than about 1 in 5,000, so it is reported as #emph[p] \< .001. Chance alone is a very poor explanation of the data, and the null hypothesis can be rejected.

A test does not always end this way, and the contrast is instructive. Chapter 5 listed GPA by gender as a comparison where no difference was expected. The same shuffling procedure gives a very different picture:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("gpa_gap ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("gpa[first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("gender ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Female\"");#NormalTok("]) ");#SpecialCharTok("-");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("gpa[first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("gender ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Male\"");#NormalTok("])");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("shuffled_gpa ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("replicate");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("5000");#NormalTok(", {");],
[#NormalTok("  shuffled ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sample");#NormalTok("(first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("gender)");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("gpa[shuffled ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Female\"");#NormalTok("]) ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("gpa[shuffled ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Male\"");#NormalTok("])");],
[#NormalTok("})");],
[#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("observed =");#NormalTok(" gpa_gap, ");#AttributeTok("p_value =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("abs");#NormalTok("(shuffled_gpa) ");#SpecialCharTok(">=");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("abs");#NormalTok("(gpa_gap)))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("   observed     p_value ");],
[#NormalTok("-0.01286325  0.63240000 ");],));
]
]
The average GPA of women and men differs by only 0.01 grade points, and more than half of the shuffles produce a difference at least that large. Such a difference is exactly what chance produces, so the data gives no reason to reject the null hypothesis.

This procedure is a #strong[permutation test]. It contains the whole logic of hypothesis testing, and every test in the rest of the chapter follows it: assume the null hypothesis, work out which results it makes likely, and see where the observed result falls. The formal tests differ mainly in using mathematics instead of shuffling to find the null distribution, which is faster and, when their assumptions hold, gives almost the same answer.

=== The p-value
<the-p-value>
The p-value is the probability of a result at least as extreme as the one observed, #emph[if the null hypothesis were true]. A small p-value means the data would be surprising in a world where nothing is going on, and the null hypothesis is #strong[rejected]. By convention, "small" means below 0.05, a threshold called the #strong[significance level], $alpha$, and such a result is called #strong[statistically significant]. A large p-value means the data is consistent with the null hypothesis, which is then #strong[not rejected]. This is not proof that the null hypothesis is true; it means only that the data does not provide strong evidence against it.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
A p-value is #strong[not] the probability that the null hypothesis is true, and #strong[not] the probability that the result happened by chance. It is the probability of data like yours, #emph[assuming] the null hypothesis is true. A p-value of 0.03 means: if the workshop had no effect, a difference at least this large would appear in only 3% of studies like this one.

]
, 
title: 
[
What a p-value is not
]
, 
background_color: 
rgb("#f7dddc")
, 
icon_color: 
rgb("#CC1914")
, 
icon: 
fa-exclamation()
, 
body_background_color: 
white
)
]
=== Type I and Type II errors
<type-i-and-type-ii-errors>
Because a test works with probabilities, its decision can be wrong in two ways, shown in #ref(<tbl-errors>, supplement: [Table]).

#figure([
#table(
  columns: 3,
  align: (auto,auto,auto,),
  table.header([], [$H_0$ is really true], [$H_0$ is really false],),
  table.hline(),
  [#strong[Reject $H_0$]], [Type I error (a false alarm)], [Correct],
  [#strong[Do not reject $H_0$]], [Correct], [Type II error (a missed effect)],
)
], caption: figure.caption(
position: top, 
[
The two kinds of error in hypothesis testing
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-tbl", 
supplement: "Table", 
)
<tbl-errors>


The significance level controls Type I errors: with $alpha = 0.05$, about 5% of tests of a true null hypothesis will be "significant" by chance alone. This can be demonstrated. The simulation below splits the students into two groups completely at random, so that no real difference can exist, tests whether their wellbeing differs, and repeats the whole procedure a thousand times:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("p_values ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("replicate");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1000");#NormalTok(", {");],
[#NormalTok("  random_group ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sample");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok("), ");#DecValTok("300");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" random_group)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("p.value");],
[#NormalTok("})");],
[#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(p_values ");#SpecialCharTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.05");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.065");],));
]
]
About 6% of the tests are "significant", even though every difference is pure chance, close to the 5% that the significance level predicts. A single significant result is therefore never the final word.

=== Statistical power
<statistical-power>
A Type II error, missing an effect that is really there, depends mainly on two things: the size of the effect and the size of the sample. A stricter significance level also makes effects harder to detect, which is one reason not to lower it without cause. The probability that a study detects an effect of a given size, when the effect is real, is its #strong[power] (Chapter 5). Power is easiest to understand by simulation. The function below imagines a study of the workshop in which the true effect is 5 points (with a standard deviation of 11, as in the real data), runs a t-test, and reports whether the result was significant. Running it 2,000 times for each group size shows how often studies of that size succeed:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("simulate_study ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#ControlFlowTok("function");#NormalTok("(n_per_group, ");#AttributeTok("effect =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("5");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("sd =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("11");#NormalTok(") {");],
[#NormalTok("  invited     ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rnorm");#NormalTok("(n_per_group, ");#AttributeTok("mean =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("60");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" effect, ");#AttributeTok("sd =");#NormalTok(" sd)");],
[#NormalTok("  not_invited ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rnorm");#NormalTok("(n_per_group, ");#AttributeTok("mean =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("60");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("sd =");#NormalTok(" sd)");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(invited, not_invited)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("p.value ");#SpecialCharTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.05");],
[#NormalTok("}");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("group_sizes ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("20");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("40");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("80");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("150");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("simulated_power ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sapply");#NormalTok("(group_sizes, \\(n) ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("replicate");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2000");#NormalTok(", ");#FunctionTok("simulate_study");#NormalTok("(n))))");],
[#FunctionTok("data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("students_per_group =");#NormalTok(" group_sizes, ");#AttributeTok("power =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(simulated_power, ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("  students_per_group power");],
[#NormalTok("1                 10  0.17");],
[#NormalTok("2                 20  0.29");],
[#NormalTok("3                 40  0.53");],
