[],
[#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(power_curve, ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" n, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" power)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_line");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"grey50\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_point");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("n =");#NormalTok(" group_sizes, ");#AttributeTok("power =");#NormalTok(" simulated_power), ");#AttributeTok("size =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_hline");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("yintercept =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.8");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("linetype =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"dashed\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Students per group\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Power\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("12");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("07-hypothesis-testing_files/figure-typst/fig-power-1.svg", alt: "A rising curve. With 10 students per group, power is about 0.16; with 80 it passes 0.8; with 150 it is close to 1. The simulated points lie on the calculated curve."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Power of a study to detect a 5-point workshop effect, by the number of students in each group: simulated (points) and calculated with power.t.test() (line).
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-power>


With 10 students per group, a real 5-point effect is detected only about 17% of the time; such a study would usually end with "no significant difference", even though the workshop works. Around 80 students per group are needed to reach the conventional 80% power, marked by the dashed line. The study's 300 per group gives power close to 100%. The line, calculated with #NormalTok("power.t.test()");, agrees with the simulation, which shows what that function computes. A non-significant result from a small study should therefore be read with great caution: it may say more about the size of the study than about the effect.

=== Multiple testing
<multiple-testing>
The 5% false alarm rate applies to #emph[each] test. A study that runs many tests will almost certainly produce some false alarms. With 20 independent tests of true null hypotheses, the chance of at least one "significant" result is

#block[
#Skylighting(([#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.95");#SpecialCharTok("^");#DecValTok("20");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.6415141");],));
]
]
about 64%. A simulation confirms it. Each simulated study below runs 20 t-tests on pure noise and records whether any of them came out significant:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("8");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("any_false_alarm ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("replicate");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1000");#NormalTok(", {");],
[#NormalTok("  p ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("replicate");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("20");#NormalTok(", ");#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("rnorm");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok("), ");#FunctionTok("rnorm");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok("))");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("p.value)");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("any");#NormalTok("(p ");#SpecialCharTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.05");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("})");],
[#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(any_false_alarm)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.653");],));
]
]
Roughly two studies in three find "something", although there is nothing to find. This is why the main hypotheses of a thesis should be stated in advance (Chapter 5), why a study that reports only its significant results misleads, and why the tests in Chapter 8 correct for multiple comparisons.

=== One-tailed and two-tailed tests
<one-tailed-and-two-tailed-tests>
A #strong[two-tailed] test looks for a difference in either direction: the workshop might raise #emph[or] lower wellbeing. A #strong[one-tailed] test looks in one direction only. One-tailed tests are easier to pass, which makes them tempting, but they are justified only when the opposite direction is truly impossible or irrelevant, and when the direction was decided before seeing the data. Two-tailed tests are the standard choice, and R's tests are two-tailed by default. The permutation test above was two-tailed as well: it counted shuffles that were extreme in either direction.

=== Statistical and practical significance
<statistical-and-practical-significance>
A statistically significant result is not necessarily an important one. With a large enough sample, even a tiny, meaningless difference becomes significant. Every result should therefore be reported with #emph[its size], not only with its significance, and the rest of this chapter does so for each test.

== The one-sample t-test
<the-one-sample-t-test>
The second research question asks whether graduate students sleep less than the recommended 7 hours (RQ2). Chapter 5 stated the hypothesis with a direction: average sleep is #emph[below] 7 hours. The test is nevertheless two-tailed, with the null hypothesis that average sleep is 7 hours and the alternative that it is not, the cautious choice explained in the section on one-tailed and two-tailed tests. The direction of the result is then read from the estimate. A #strong[one-sample t-test] compares a sample average with a fixed value, given by the argument #NormalTok("mu");:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("sleep_test ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("sleep_hours, ");#AttributeTok("mu =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("sleep_test");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    One Sample t-test");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  first_sem$sleep_hours");],
[#NormalTok("t = -12.177, df = 593, p-value < 2.2e-16");],
[#NormalTok("alternative hypothesis: true mean is not equal to 7");],
[#NormalTok("95 percent confidence interval:");],
[#NormalTok(" 6.400196 6.566808");],
[#NormalTok("sample estimates:");],
[#NormalTok("mean of x ");],
[#NormalTok(" 6.483502 ");],));
]
]
The output contains three results. The estimate is the sample average, 6.48 hours. The confidence interval runs from 6.40 to 6.57 hours and does not include 7. The test itself reports the #emph[t] statistic, which measures how far the average lies from 7 in units of standard errors (here 12.2 standard errors below), and a p-value so small that R prints it as "\< 2.2e-16", meaning less than 0.0000000000000002.

Graduate students sleep about half an hour less than recommended, and the difference is far too large to be chance. The confidence interval also answers the practical question: the shortfall is somewhere between about 26 and 36 minutes a night.

