== The two-sample t-test
<sec-comparing-groups>
The workshop question (RQ3) compares two independent groups: the students invited at random to the six-week workshop after their first semester, and those who were not invited. The permutation test has already answered it; the two-sample t-test reaches the same answer with a formula.

=== A small example
<a-small-example>
A small invented example shows the mechanics. Suppose there were only five students in each group, with these wellbeing scores out of 100:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("invited     ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("68");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("72");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("65");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("75");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("70");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("not_invited ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("62");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("66");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("60");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("69");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("64");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(invited)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 70");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(not_invited)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 64.2");],));
]
]
The invited group is 5.8 points higher on average. A #strong[two-sample t-test] assesses whether a difference between the averages of two independent groups is larger than chance would produce:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("small_test ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(invited, not_invited)");],
[#NormalTok("small_test");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Welch Two Sample t-test");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  invited and not_invited");],
[#NormalTok("t = 2.5099, df = 7.9411, p-value = 0.03658");],
[#NormalTok("alternative hypothesis: true difference in means is not equal to 0");],
[#NormalTok("95 percent confidence interval:");],
[#NormalTok("  0.4642934 11.1357066");],
[#NormalTok("sample estimates:");],
[#NormalTok("mean of x mean of y ");],
[#NormalTok("     70.0      64.2 ");],));
]
]
The p-value is 0.037: if the workshop had no effect, a difference this large would appear in about 4 studies in 100. By the usual 0.05 threshold the result is statistically significant, although with only five students per group it is fragile evidence.

=== Testing the workshop
<testing-the-workshop>
The real study uses the #NormalTok("semester2"); table built for the permutation test. Before any test, the data should be inspected, and #ref(<fig-workshop>, supplement: [Figure]) shows wellbeing in each group.

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(semester2, ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" workshop, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" wellbeing, ");#AttributeTok("fill =");#NormalTok(" workshop)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("geom_boxplot");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("show.legend =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("FALSE");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("width =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.5");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("NULL");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Wellbeing (0 to 100)\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("13");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("07-hypothesis-testing_files/figure-typst/fig-workshop-1.svg", alt: "Box plot of wellbeing scores for invited and not-invited students. The invited group's box sits a few points higher."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Wellbeing at the end of semester 2, by workshop group. Each box shows the middle half of the students; the line inside is the median.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-workshop>


The invited group sits a little higher, but the groups overlap considerably. In the test, the formula #NormalTok("wellbeing ~ workshop"); reads "wellbeing by workshop group":

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("result ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" workshop, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" semester2)");],
[#NormalTok("result");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Welch Two Sample t-test");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  wellbeing by workshop");],
[#NormalTok("t = 5.469, df = 597.52, p-value = 6.66e-08");],
[#NormalTok("alternative hypothesis: true difference in means between group Invited and group Not invited is not equal to 0");],
[#NormalTok("95 percent confidence interval:");],
[#NormalTok(" 3.364699 7.135301");],
[#NormalTok("sample estimates:");],
[#NormalTok("    mean in group Invited mean in group Not invited ");],
[#NormalTok("                    65.47                     60.22 ");],));
]
]
Invited students scored 5.2 points higher on average (65.5 against 60.2). The 95% confidence interval for the difference runs from 3.4 to 7.1 points, and the p-value is below 0.001, in agreement with the permutation test. Because students were invited #strong[at random], the study can conclude that the workshop #emph[caused] the improvement, not merely that it accompanied it: randomisation makes the two groups alike in everything except the invitation (Chapter 5).

The heading of the output, "Welch Two Sample t-test", names the version R uses by default. It does not assume that the two groups have equal spread, which makes it the safer choice and the appropriate default.

=== Effect size
<effect-size>
A difference of five points on a 100-point scale is hard to judge on its own. A common standard is #strong[Cohen's #emph[d]]: the difference between the means divided by the standard deviation. It expresses the difference in standard deviations, so it can be compared across studies and scales:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("inv ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("wellbeing[semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("workshop ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Invited\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("not ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("wellbeing[semester2");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("workshop ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Not invited\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("d ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" (");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(inv) ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(not)) ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sqrt");#NormalTok("((");#FunctionTok("var");#NormalTok("(inv) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("var");#NormalTok("(not)) ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("d");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.4465413");],));
]
]
Cohen's guidelines call 0.2 a small effect, 0.5 medium, and 0.8 large @cohen1988. At about 0.45, the workshop's effect is small to medium: a real improvement, but not a transformation. For a six-week workshop, that is a worthwhile result, and exactly the kind of judgement a thesis discussion should make.

== The paired t-test
<the-paired-t-test>
The two-sample test compared two different groups of students. A different question concerns change within the same people: whether the invited students' own wellbeing rose from semester 1 to semester 2. When the same students are measured twice, the two sets of scores are #strong[paired]. Each student is compared with themselves, which removes the large differences #emph[between] students and makes the test more sensitive.

The data needs one row per student, with the two semesters side by side (Chapter 3):

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(tidyr)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("invited_wide ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" semesters ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(semester ");#SpecialCharTok("%in%");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(students, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(workshop ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Invited\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(student_id, semester, wellbeing) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("pivot_wider");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("names_from =");#NormalTok(" semester, ");#AttributeTok("values_from =");#NormalTok(" wellbeing,");],
[#NormalTok("              ");#AttributeTok("names_prefix =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"semester_\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(invited_wide");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("semester_2, invited_wide");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("semester_1, ");#AttributeTok("paired =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Paired t-test");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  invited_wide$semester_2 and invited_wide$semester_1");],
[#NormalTok("t = 10.936, df = 299, p-value < 2.2e-16");],
[#NormalTok("alternative hypothesis: true mean difference is not equal to 0");],
[#NormalTok("95 percent confidence interval:");],
[#NormalTok(" 3.955360 5.691307");],
[#NormalTok("sample estimates:");],
[#NormalTok("mean difference ");],
[#NormalTok("       4.823333 ");],));
]
]
The invited students' wellbeing rose by 4.8 points on average, and the change is clearly significant.

This test cannot say #emph[why] wellbeing rose. The rise could come from the workshop, or semester 2 could simply be less stressful than semester 1 for everyone. The paired test has no comparison group; it is the two-sample test, comparing invited with not-invited students, that answers the causal question. Choosing the design that actually answers the question matters as much as running the test correctly.

== Assumptions of the t-test
<assumptions-of-the-t-test>
Every test rests on assumptions, and a result is only as trustworthy as the assumptions behind it. The first assumption of the t-test is #strong[independence]: each observation comes from a different person, or, for the paired test, each pair does. Independence is a property of the study design rather than of the numbers, so it cannot be tested; it must be secured when the data is collected, which is why the tests in this chapter use one value per student. The second is #strong[approximate normality] of the data in each group. Thanks to the central limit theorem, this matters little in large samples: with 30 or more students per group, moderate skewness is not a problem. The third, #strong[similar spread] in the two groups, is required only by the classic version of the two-sample test; Welch's version, R's default, does not need it.

Normality is best judged with graphs: a histogram and a Q-Q plot (Chapter 6). Formal tests of normality exist, such as the Shapiro-Wilk test, but they contain a trap:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("shapiro.test");#NormalTok("(first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("sleep_hours)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Shapiro-Wilk normality test");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  first_sem$sleep_hours");],
[#NormalTok("W = 0.99291, p-value = 0.006536");],));
]
]
The test declares that sleep is #emph[not] normal (p \< 0.05), yet in Chapter 6 the histogram and Q-Q plot of sleep looked almost perfectly normal. Both are right. With 600 students, the test is sensitive enough to detect tiny departures from normality that make no practical difference. In large samples, normality tests reject almost everything; in small samples, where normality matters most, they can miss real problems. The graphs, together with the sample size, are the better guide.

== Non-parametric tests
<non-parametric-tests>
When data is clearly not normal and the sample is small, or when the data is ordinal (ranks, or answers on a short scale), #strong[non-parametric tests] offer an alternative. Instead of the values themselves, they compare the #strong[ranks] of the values, so extreme values and skewness do not distort them. The price is some loss of power when the data is in fact close to normal.

The #strong[Mann-Whitney U test], called the Wilcoxon rank-sum test in R, replaces the two-sample t-test. Caffeine is strongly skewed, which makes the comparison of caffeine intake between women and men a suitable case:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("tapply");#NormalTok("(first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("caffeine_mg, first_sem");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("gender, median, ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Female   Male ");],
[#NormalTok(" 152.5  170.0 ");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("wilcox.test");#NormalTok("(caffeine_mg ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" gender, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" first_sem)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Wilcoxon rank sum test with continuity correction");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  caffeine_mg by gender");],
[#NormalTok("W = 40551, p-value = 0.06319");],
[#NormalTok("alternative hypothesis: true location shift is not equal to 0");],));
]
]
The medians differ a little, but the p-value is above 0.05, so there is no clear evidence of a difference. Medians, rather than means, should be reported alongside a non-parametric test.

The #strong[Wilcoxon signed-rank test] replaces the paired t-test. Applied to the invited students' wellbeing in semesters 1 and 2, it gives:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("wilcox.test");#NormalTok("(invited_wide");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("semester_2, invited_wide");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("semester_1, ");#AttributeTok("paired =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Wilcoxon signed rank test with continuity correction");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  invited_wide$semester_2 and invited_wide$semester_1");],
[#NormalTok("V = 33026, p-value < 2.2e-16");],
[#NormalTok("alternative hypothesis: true location shift is not equal to 0");],));
]
]
The result agrees with the paired t-test. When the parametric and non-parametric tests agree, either can be reported with confidence; when they disagree, the data deserves a second look, and the test whose assumptions fit should be trusted.

== Chi-square tests
<chi-square-tests>
The t-test compares averages of a numeric variable. For categorical variables, the #strong[chi-square test] ($chi^2$) compares counts: how many cases fall into each category, against how many would be expected if the null hypothesis were true.

=== Test of expected proportions
<test-of-expected-proportions>
The first form of the test, usually called the #strong[goodness-of-fit test], compares the counts of one categorical variable with proportions stated in advance. Whether the sample is evenly split between women and men is a simple example:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("table");#NormalTok("(students");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("gender)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Female   Male ");],
[#NormalTok("   312    288 ");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("chisq.test");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("table");#NormalTok("(students");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("gender), ");#AttributeTok("p =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("0.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("0.5");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Chi-squared test for given probabilities");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  table(students$gender)");],
[#NormalTok("X-squared = 0.96, df = 1, p-value = 0.3272");],));
]
]
With 312 women and 288 men, the split is a little uneven, but the p-value is large: a difference of this size is quite likely in a random sample from an evenly split population. There is no evidence against a 50/50 split, and a non-significant result of this kind is still a result.

=== Test of independence
<test-of-independence>
The #strong[test of independence] examines whether two categorical variables are related, such as having a job and considering dropping out:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("jobs ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("table");#NormalTok("(students");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("employment, students");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("considering_dropout)");],
[#NormalTok("jobs");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("               ");],
[#NormalTok("                 No Yes");],
[#NormalTok("  Full-time job  72  25");],
[#NormalTok("  None          265  42");],
[#NormalTok("  Part-time job 173  23");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("prop.table");#NormalTok("(jobs, ");#AttributeTok("margin =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("               ");],
[#NormalTok("                  No  Yes");],
[#NormalTok("  Full-time job 0.74 0.26");],
[#NormalTok("  None          0.86 0.14");],
[#NormalTok("  Part-time job 0.88 0.12");],));
]
]
Dividing by the row totals, with #NormalTok("prop.table(..., margin = 1)");, turns the counts into proportions within each row. About a quarter of students with full-time jobs have considered dropping out, compared with about one in eight of the others. The test shows whether this difference exceeds what chance would produce:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("job_test ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("chisq.test");#NormalTok("(jobs)");],
[#NormalTok("job_test");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Pearson's Chi-squared test");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  jobs");],
[#NormalTok("X-squared = 10.888, df = 2, p-value = 0.004322");],));
]
]
With a p-value of 0.004, the difference is statistically significant: students with full-time jobs are more likely to consider dropping out. The result is an association, not proof that jobs cause thoughts of dropping out, since students who work full-time may differ in other ways too, such as financial pressure.

The chi-square test is reliable when the #strong[expected] counts, the counts that would appear if the variables were unrelated, are at least 5 in every cell. The test stores them:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(job_test");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("expected, ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("               ");],
[#NormalTok("                   No  Yes");],
[#NormalTok("  Full-time job  82.4 14.6");],
[#NormalTok("  None          261.0 46.0");],
[#NormalTok("  Part-time job 166.6 29.4");],));
]
]
All are well above 5. When some are not, Fisher's exact test, #NormalTok("fisher.test()");, is the appropriate alternative for small counts.

