Each row compares two faculties: the difference in average stress, its confidence interval, and an adjusted p-value (#NormalTok("p adj");). Only one comparison is significant: Health Sciences students are more stressed than Humanities students, by about 0.34 points on the 1-to-5 scale. The other differences are small enough to be chance.

=== Assumptions of ANOVA
<assumptions-of-anova>
ANOVA makes the same assumptions as the t-test: independent observations, roughly normal data within each group (or large groups), and similar spread in each group. The spread can be checked with #strong[Levene's test], from the #strong[car] package:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(car)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Loading required package: carData");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Attaching package: 'car'");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("The following object is masked from 'package:dplyr':");],
[],
[#NormalTok("    recode");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("leveneTest");#NormalTok("(stress ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(faculty), ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Levene's Test for Homogeneity of Variance (center = median)");],
[#NormalTok("       Df F value Pr(>F)");],
[#NormalTok("group   4  0.9429 0.4386");],
[#NormalTok("      595               ");],));
]
]
The p-value is large, so there is no evidence that the spread differs between faculties. When the assumptions fail, the #strong[Kruskal-Wallis test] is the non-parametric alternative, comparing ranks instead of averages:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("kruskal.test");#NormalTok("(stress ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" faculty, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Kruskal-Wallis rank sum test");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  stress by faculty");],
[#NormalTok("Kruskal-Wallis chi-squared = 15.46, df = 4, p-value = 0.003836");],));
]
]
It agrees with the ANOVA.

== Two-way ANOVA
<two-way-anova>
Wellbeing might depend on the programme (Master's or PhD), on study mode (full-time or part-time), or on a combination of both. A #strong[two-way ANOVA] examines all three possibilities at once. The #strong[main effect] of programme is the difference between Master's and PhD students on average, and the main effect of study mode is the difference between full-time and part-time students. The #strong[interaction] asks whether the effect of study mode #emph[depends on] the programme: part-time study might, for example, be harder on PhD students than on Master's students.

An #strong[interaction plot] shows the group averages as lines. If the lines are parallel, there is no interaction: the effect of study mode is the same in both programmes.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("study ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("summarise");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("wellbeing =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(wellbeing), ");#AttributeTok(".by =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(programme, study_mode)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" programme, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" wellbeing, ");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" study_mode, ");#AttributeTok("group =");#NormalTok(" study_mode)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
