<fig-violations>


A U-shaped or curved pattern means that the model has missed a curve, and a squared term or a transformation may be needed. A funnel means that the spread is not constant, which makes standard errors, and therefore p-values and confidence intervals, unreliable. With these pictures in mind, the real model can be checked. Calling #NormalTok("plot()"); on a model draws four diagnostic plots, of which the first two matter most:

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("par");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("mfrow =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("))");],
[#FunctionTok("plot");#NormalTok("(gpa_model, ");#AttributeTok("which =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("par");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("mfrow =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("))");],));
#figure([
#box(image("08-anova-regression_files/figure-typst/fig-diagnostics-1.svg", alt: "Left: residuals scattered evenly around zero across all fitted values, with no pattern. Right: residual Q-Q plot with points close to the diagonal."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Diagnostic plots for the GPA model: residuals against fitted values (left), and a Q-Q plot of the residuals (right).
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-diagnostics>


The left plot resembles the first simulated panel: a shapeless band around zero, with no curve and no funnel. In the right plot, the residuals follow the line, so they are close to normal. Both look good for the GPA model. Points far off the line in either plot would point to unusual cases worth looking at.

