== Logistic regression
<logistic-regression>
The third research question concerns a yes-or-no outcome: whether a student has considered dropping out. Linear regression is the wrong tool here, because it could predict probabilities below 0 or above 1, which make no sense. #strong[Logistic regression] models the probability of a "yes" in a way that always stays between 0 and 1.

=== Odds and odds ratios
<odds-and-odds-ratios>
Logistic regression works with #strong[odds] rather than probabilities. The odds of an event are the probability that it happens divided by the probability that it does not. The table from Chapter 7 shows how they are calculated:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("jobs ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("table");#NormalTok("(students");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("employment, students");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("considering_dropout)");],
[#NormalTok("jobs");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("               ");],
[#NormalTok("                 No Yes");],
[#NormalTok("  Full-time job  72  25");],
[#NormalTok("  None          265  42");],
[#NormalTok("  Part-time job 173  23");],));
]
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("odds_full ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" jobs[");#StringTok("\"Full-time job\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok("] ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" jobs[");#StringTok("\"Full-time job\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("odds_none ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" jobs[");#StringTok("\"None\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok("] ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" jobs[");#StringTok("\"None\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok("]");],
[#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("full_time_job =");#NormalTok(" odds_full, ");#AttributeTok("no_job =");#NormalTok(" odds_none, ");#AttributeTok("odds_ratio =");#NormalTok(" odds_full ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" odds_none)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("full_time_job        no_job    odds_ratio ");],
[#NormalTok("    0.3472222     0.1584906     2.1908069 ");],));
]
]
Among students with a full-time job, 25 have considered dropping out and 72 have not, which gives odds of about 0.35. Among students without a job, the odds are 42 to 265, about 0.16. The #strong[odds ratio] compares the two: students with a full-time job have about 2.2 times the odds of considering dropout. An odds ratio of 1 means no difference, above 1 means higher odds, and below 1 means lower odds.

=== A model for dropout
<a-model-for-dropout>
The function #NormalTok("glm()");, for #emph[generalised linear model], fits logistic regression when told #NormalTok("family = binomial");. The outcome must be coded 0 and 1:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("study ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" study ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("dropout =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("as.integer");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[],
[#NormalTok("dropout_model ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("glm");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  dropout ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" stress ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" support ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" financial_worry ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" employment ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" study_mode,");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" study, ");#AttributeTok("family =");#NormalTok(" binomial");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(dropout_model)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                          Estimate Std. Error   z value     Pr(>|z|)");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)             -4.0185658  1.2316928 -3.262636 1.103811e-03");],
[#NormalTok("stress                   1.2822937  0.2398794  5.345576 9.012973e-08");],
[#NormalTok("support                 -1.1102419  0.2039547 -5.443572 5.222263e-08");],
[#NormalTok("financial_worry          0.4537396  0.1216263  3.730605 1.910209e-04");],
[#NormalTok("employmentNone          -0.5892840  0.4378768 -1.345776 1.783748e-01");],
[#NormalTok("employmentPart-time job -0.5387011  0.4522237 -1.191227 2.335644e-01");],
[#NormalTok("study_modePart-time      0.2763818  0.3674127  0.752238 4.519079e-01");],));
]
]
The coefficients are on the scale of #strong[log-odds], which is hard to interpret directly. The function #NormalTok("exp()"); turns them into odds ratios, and #NormalTok("confint.default()"); gives their confidence intervals:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("exp");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("cbind");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("odds_ratio =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("coef");#NormalTok("(dropout_model), ");#FunctionTok("confint.default");#NormalTok("(dropout_model))) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                        odds_ratio 2.5 % 97.5 %");],
[#NormalTok("(Intercept)                   0.02  0.00   0.20");],
[#NormalTok("stress                        3.60  2.25   5.77");],
[#NormalTok("support                       0.33  0.22   0.49");],
[#NormalTok("financial_worry               1.57  1.24   2.00");],
[#NormalTok("employmentNone                0.55  0.24   1.31");],
[#NormalTok("employmentPart-time job       0.58  0.24   1.42");],
[#NormalTok("study_modePart-time           1.32  0.64   2.71");],));
]
]
Holding the other predictors constant, each extra point of #strong[stress] multiplies the odds of considering dropout by about 3.6, and each extra point of #strong[financial worry] by about 1.6. Each extra point of #strong[supervisor support] multiplies them by about 0.33, which cuts them by about 67%. The hypothesis stated in Chapter 5, that higher stress raises the odds of considering dropout, is supported: the confidence interval for stress lies entirely above 1.

Something interesting has happened to #strong[employment]. In Chapter 7, and in the odds ratio above, students with full-time jobs had about twice the odds of considering dropping out. In this model, the confidence intervals for both employment categories include 1: once stress and financial worry are taken into account, having a job adds little on its own. The likely explanation is that a full-time job matters #emph[because] of the stress and money pressure that come with it. This is the kind of insight multiple regression makes possible.

=== Predicted probabilities
<predicted-probabilities>
Odds ratios are hard for many readers; predicted probabilities are easier. The model can predict the probability of considering dropout for two example students who are identical except for their stress and support:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("examples ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("tibble");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("stress          =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("2.5");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.0");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("support         =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("4.0");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("2.0");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("financial_worry =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("employment      =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"None\"");#NormalTok(",");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#AttributeTok("study_mode      =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Full-time\"");],
[#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("predict");#NormalTok("(dropout_model, ");#AttributeTok("newdata =");#NormalTok(" examples, ");#AttributeTok("type =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"response\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("   1    2 ");],
[#NormalTok("0.01 0.42 ");],));
]
]
The argument #NormalTok("type = \"response\""); asks for probabilities rather than log-odds. A student with low stress and good support has about a 1% chance of considering dropout; a stressed student with little support, about 42%. For a thesis's recommendations, that contrast is more persuasive than any coefficient.

Whether the model can #emph[predict] who will consider dropping out, and how that should be tested fairly, is a different question, about prediction rather than explanation. It is the subject of Chapters 11 and 12.

