== Constructs and latent variables
<constructs-and-latent-variables>
A single questionnaire item is a poor measure of a construct. The answer to "I feel unable to control important things in my studies" depends on the student's stress, but also on how they read the question that day, on their mood, and on how they use the answer scale. Each item therefore carries two things: a signal from the construct, and noise of its own. A #strong[latent variable] is the construct thought to lie behind the items: it is not observed, but it is assumed to cause part of every answer.

Averaging several items keeps the signal, which all items share, and lets the noise, which differs from item to item, partly cancel out. A simulation shows how much this helps. It creates a "true" stress level for 600 imaginary students, then six items, each equal to the true level plus its own random noise, and compares how closely one item and the average of several items follow the true level:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("42");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("true_stress ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rnorm");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("600");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("simulated_items ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sapply");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(", ");#ControlFlowTok("function");#NormalTok("(i) true_stress ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rnorm");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("600");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("sd =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("))");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("sapply");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(", ");#ControlFlowTok("function");#NormalTok("(k) ");#FunctionTok("cor");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("rowMeans");#NormalTok("(simulated_items[, ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#NormalTok("k, ");#AttributeTok("drop =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("FALSE");#NormalTok("]), true_stress)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("setNames");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("paste");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"items\"");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("1 items 2 items 3 items 4 items 5 items 6 items ");],
[#NormalTok("   0.68    0.81    0.86    0.89    0.91    0.92 ");],));
]
]
In this simulation, one item correlates only about 0.68 with the true stress level, while the average of six items correlates about 0.92. This is why questionnaires use several items for each construct, and why the items are combined into a scale score (Chapter 3).

The approach rests on an assumption that must be checked: that the items of a scale really reflect one common construct, and not several. If some stress items actually measured tiredness, averaging them would mix two things. Chapter 5 called this #strong[construct validity]. The methods in this chapter provide evidence for it: factor analysis shows whether the items group as intended, and Cronbach's alpha shows whether the items of each group agree with each other, which Chapter 5 called #strong[internal consistency].

== Many variables at once
<many-variables-at-once>
The 22 questionnaire items are in #NormalTok("questionnaire");:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(dplyr)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Attaching package: 'dplyr'");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("The following objects are masked from 'package:stats':");],
[],
[#NormalTok("    filter, lag");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("The following objects are masked from 'package:base':");],
[],
[#NormalTok("    intersect, setdiff, setequal, union");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(ggplot2)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("items ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" questionnaire ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok("student_id)");],
[#FunctionTok("ncol");#NormalTok("(items)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 22");],));
]
]
With 22 items, there are 231 correlations between pairs of items, far too many to read one by one. A picture helps. The #strong[corrplot] package draws a correlation matrix as a grid of coloured squares, and #NormalTok("order = \"hclust\""); sorts the items so that those that correlate strongly sit together:

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(corrplot)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("corrplot 0.95 loaded");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("corrplot");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("cor");#NormalTok("(items, ");#AttributeTok("use =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"pairwise.complete.obs\"");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("         ");#AttributeTok("method =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"color\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("order =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"hclust\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("tl.col =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"black\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("tl.cex =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.7");#NormalTok(")");],));
#figure([
#box(image("09-multivariate_files/figure-typst/fig-corrplot-1.svg", alt: "A 22 by 22 grid of coloured squares. Four blocks of strongly correlated items stand out along the diagonal, one for each scale; stress and burnout items also correlate with each other."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Correlations among the 22 questionnaire items, sorted so that similar items are next to each other.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-corrplot>


Blocks of related items stand out along the diagonal, one for each scale, just as the idea of latent variables predicts: items that share a construct correlate with each other. Blue squares show positive correlations and red negative. The stress and burnout blocks also correlate with each other, and one item, #NormalTok("stress_4");, is red against the other stress items, because it is worded the other way round. The methods in this chapter turn this picture into numbers.

== Principal component analysis
<principal-component-analysis>
#strong[Principal component analysis] (PCA) replaces many correlated variables with a few new ones, called #strong[principal components], that together keep most of the information. The first component is the combination of the variables that captures as much of their variation as possible. The second captures as much as possible of what is left, and so on.

Two items that correlate strongly, such as "I feel emotionally drained by my studies" and "I feel exhausted when I think about my thesis", illustrate the idea. Most students who score high on one score high on the other, so one combined score, their average for example, keeps most of what the two items tell. PCA does this for all the variables at once, and finds the best combinations automatically.

=== Running PCA
<running-pca>
The function #NormalTok("prcomp()"); runs PCA, and #NormalTok("scale. = TRUE"); puts every variable on the same scale first, which is almost always wanted. PCA needs complete data, so #NormalTok("na.omit()"); keeps only the students who answered every item:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("items_complete ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("na.omit");#NormalTok("(items)");],
[#FunctionTok("nrow");#NormalTok("(items_complete)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 396");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("pca ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("prcomp");#NormalTok("(items_complete, ");#AttributeTok("scale. =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(pca)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("importance[, ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok("] ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("                         PC1   PC2   PC3   PC4   PC5   PC6");],
[#NormalTok("Standard deviation     2.580 1.694 1.250 1.110 0.899 0.852");],
[#NormalTok("Proportion of Variance 0.302 0.130 0.071 0.056 0.037 0.033");],
[#NormalTok("Cumulative Proportion  0.302 0.433 0.504 0.560 0.597 0.630");],));
]
]
Only 396 of the 600 students answered all 22 items: a small share of missing answers per item adds up to many incomplete students. Factor analysis, below, can use the incomplete students too.

The table shows how much of the total variation each component captures. The first component alone captures 30%, and the first four together 56%.

=== The number of components
<the-number-of-components>
No single rule decides how many components to keep, so researchers use several. The #strong[Kaiser rule] keeps components whose #strong[eigenvalue], the amount of variation they capture, is greater than 1, that is, more than one original variable's worth; here, 4 components pass. The #strong[scree plot] shows the eigenvalues in order, and the researcher looks for the "elbow" where the line flattens out. #strong[Parallel analysis] compares the eigenvalues with those from random data of the same size, and keeps the components that beat random data; it is the most reliable of the three.

The #strong[factoextra] package draws a scree plot directly:

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(factoextra)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Welcome! Want to learn more? See two factoextra-related books at https://goo.gl/ve3WBa");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("fviz_eig");#NormalTok("(pca, ");#AttributeTok("addlabels =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("ncp =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in geom_bar(stat = \"identity\", fill = barfill, color = barcolor, :");],
[#NormalTok("Ignoring empty aesthetic: `width`.");],));
]
#figure([
#box(image("09-multivariate_files/figure-typst/fig-scree-1.svg", alt: "Bar chart with a line, falling steeply from the first component to the fourth, then flattening from the fifth onwards."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Scree plot: the share of variation captured by each principal component.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-scree>


The line drops steeply for four components and flattens after that. Parallel analysis, from the #strong[psych] package, agrees:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(psych)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Attaching package: 'psych'");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("The following objects are masked from 'package:ggplot2':");],
[],
[#NormalTok("    %+%, alpha");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("parallel ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fa.parallel");#NormalTok("(items, ");#AttributeTok("fa =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"fa\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("plot =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("FALSE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Parallel analysis suggests that the number of factors =  4  and the number of components =  NA ");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("parallel");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("nfact");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 4");],));
]
]
All three methods point to four dimensions, matching the four scales the questionnaire was designed to measure.

=== PCA and factor analysis compared
<pca-and-factor-analysis-compared>
PCA and factor analysis are often confused, and many theses use one when they mean the other. The difference lies in what they assume. #strong[PCA] simply summarises the variables: the components are combinations of the items, with no claim about why the items are related, and it is used to reduce many variables to a few, for example before a further analysis. #strong[Factor analysis] assumes the model of the first section: that each item is #emph[caused] by one or more latent variables, called #strong[factors], plus error of its own. A student answers the stress items the way they do #emph[because] they are stressed. Factor analysis is therefore the method for checking whether a questionnaire measures the constructs it is meant to, and that is the study's question.

== Factor analysis
<factor-analysis>
#strong[Exploratory factor analysis] (EFA) estimates how strongly each item is related to each factor. These relationships are called #strong[loadings]. Loadings run roughly from −1 to 1: close to 0 means that the item is unrelated to the factor, and a value above about 0.4 in size is usually considered a clear relationship.

The function #NormalTok("fa()"); from the psych package runs the analysis. Its main arguments are the number of factors (four, from parallel analysis), the #strong[rotation], and the estimation method. Rotation turns the factors to make them easier to interpret; "oblimin" allows the factors to correlate with each other, which is realistic, since stress and burnout surely go together. The option #NormalTok("fm = \"ml\""); uses maximum likelihood, a common estimation method:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("efa ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fa");#NormalTok("(items, ");#AttributeTok("nfactors =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("rotate =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"oblimin\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("fm =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"ml\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Loading required namespace: GPArotation");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("print");#NormalTok("(efa");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("loadings, ");#AttributeTok("cutoff =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.3");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("sort =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("Loadings:");],
[#NormalTok("               ML1    ML3    ML2    ML4   ");],
[#NormalTok("support_1       0.749                     ");],
[#NormalTok("support_2       0.718                     ");],
[#NormalTok("support_3       0.669                     ");],
[#NormalTok("support_4       0.638                     ");],
[#NormalTok("support_5       0.726                     ");],
[#NormalTok("support_6       0.619                     ");],
[#NormalTok("stress_1               0.623              ");],
[#NormalTok("stress_3               0.627              ");],
[#NormalTok("stress_4              -0.503              ");],
[#NormalTok("stress_5               0.827              ");],
[#NormalTok("stress_6               0.547              ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_1                     0.647       ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_2                     0.719       ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_4                     0.622       ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_5                     0.544       ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_6                     0.675       ");],
[#NormalTok("satisfaction_1                       0.659");],
[#NormalTok("satisfaction_2                       0.684");],
[#NormalTok("satisfaction_3                       0.585");],
[#NormalTok("satisfaction_4                       0.673");],
[#NormalTok("stress_2               0.496              ");],
[#NormalTok("burnout_3              0.325  0.446       ");],
[],
[#NormalTok("                ML1  ML3   ML2   ML4");],
[#NormalTok("SS loadings    2.87 2.41 2.384 1.735");],
[#NormalTok("Proportion Var 0.13 0.11 0.108 0.079");],
[#NormalTok("Cumulative Var 0.13 0.24 0.348 0.427");],));
]
]
In the printout, #NormalTok("cutoff = 0.3"); hides small loadings so that the pattern is easy to see, and #NormalTok("sort = TRUE"); groups the items by the factor they load on most. Unlike PCA, #NormalTok("fa()"); uses every student, including those who skipped an item.

The table shows four clear factors. Each collects the items of one scale: the six support items on one, the stress items on another, the burnout items on a third, and the four satisfaction items on the fourth. The names ML1 to ML4 are only labels; naming the factors is the researcher's job. The reversed item, #NormalTok("stress_4"); ("I feel confident handling problems in my studies"), loads #emph[negatively] on the stress factor, which is exactly right for a reversed item: agreeing with it means less stress, and it is why the item must be reversed before the stress score is calculated (Chapter 3). One item, #NormalTok("burnout_3"); ("Deadlines make me feel overwhelmed"), loads on both the burnout and the stress factor. Reading the item, that makes sense: feeling overwhelmed by deadlines is both. Such a #strong[cross-loading] item is worth discussing in a thesis, and sometimes worth rewording or removing in future studies.

Because the rotation allowed the factors to correlate, #NormalTok("fa()"); also estimates how strongly they do:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(efa");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("Phi, ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("      ML1   ML3   ML2   ML4");],
[#NormalTok("ML1  1.00 -0.37 -0.23  0.48");],
[#NormalTok("ML3 -0.37  1.00  0.66 -0.44");],
[#NormalTok("ML2 -0.23  0.66  1.00 -0.39");],
[#NormalTok("ML4  0.48 -0.44 -0.39  1.00");],));
]
]
The stress and burnout factors correlate strongly (about 0.66), but not so strongly that they are the same thing. The support and satisfaction factors correlate positively with each other and negatively with stress and burnout. These relationships make sense, which is itself evidence that the scales measure what they should.

=== Reliability and Cronbach's alpha
<reliability-and-cronbachs-alpha>
Factor analysis shows that the items of each scale belong together. #strong[Reliability] asks a related question: whether the items of a scale give consistent results (Chapter 5). The most common measure of this internal consistency is #strong[Cronbach's alpha], which ranges from 0 to 1. It rises when the items correlate strongly with each other, and also when there are more items, for the reason shown by the simulation at the start of the chapter. As a rough guide, 0.7 or above is acceptable and 0.8 or above good for research use. The function #NormalTok("alpha()"); from the psych package calculates it, after reversed items have been reversed:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("q ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" questionnaire");],
[#NormalTok("q");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("stress_4 ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" q");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("stress_4");],
[],
[#NormalTok("psych");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("alpha");#NormalTok("(q[, ");#FunctionTok("paste0");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"stress_\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(")])");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("total");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("raw_alpha");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.826084");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("psych");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("alpha");#NormalTok("(q[, ");#FunctionTok("paste0");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"burnout_\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(")])");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("total");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("raw_alpha");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.8313142");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("psych");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("alpha");#NormalTok("(q[, ");#FunctionTok("paste0");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"support_\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(")])");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("total");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("raw_alpha");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.8454391");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("psych");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("alpha");#NormalTok("(q[, ");#FunctionTok("paste0");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"satisfaction_\"");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(")])");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("total");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("raw_alpha");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 0.7642397");],));
]
]
All four scales are reliable, with alphas between 0.76 and 0.85. The full output of #NormalTok("alpha()"); also shows what alpha would be if each item were dropped, which helps to spot a weak item.

The code writes #NormalTok("psych::alpha()"); rather than #NormalTok("alpha()"); because ggplot2 also has a function called #NormalTok("alpha()");, for making colours transparent. Whichever package is loaded last wins, so after loading ggplot2 (or a package that loads it, such as factoextra) a plain #NormalTok("alpha()"); no longer calculates Cronbach's alpha. The #NormalTok("package::function()"); form always calls the intended function, whether or not the package is loaded.

A high alpha shows consistency, not validity. A scale can be highly reliable and still measure the wrong thing, as the target picture in Chapter 5 showed; the factor structure and the relationships with other constructs are the evidence for validity.

#block[
#callout(
body: 
[
A typical report: "An exploratory factor analysis (maximum likelihood, oblimin rotation) supported four factors, as indicated by parallel analysis. All items loaded on their intended factor (loadings 0.45 to 0.83 in absolute value), with one item (#emph[burnout\_3]) also loading on the stress factor. Internal consistency was acceptable to good (Cronbach's α = 0.76 to 0.85)."

A further step, #strong[confirmatory factor analysis], tests whether a structure decided in advance fits the data, rather than exploring it. It is done with the #strong[lavaan] package and is beyond this book.

]
