=== Hierarchical clustering
<hierarchical-clustering>
#strong[Hierarchical clustering] takes a different approach. It starts with every student as their own cluster and repeatedly merges the two most similar clusters, until everyone is in one. The result is a tree, called a #strong[dendrogram], which can be cut at any height to give any number of clusters, without choosing $k$ in advance. Ward's method (#NormalTok("\"ward.D2\"");) merges clusters so that they stay as compact as possible:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tree ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("hclust");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("dist");#NormalTok("(profile_data), ");#AttributeTok("method =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"ward.D2\"");#NormalTok(")");],
[#FunctionTok("fviz_dend");#NormalTok("(tree, ");#AttributeTok("k =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("show_labels =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("FALSE");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("rect =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
