== Data for prediction
<data-for-prediction>
A predictive model can only use information that would be available when the prediction is made. The university wants to act at the end of the first semester, so the model may use the students' background, their questionnaire scores from the start of the year, and their first-semester records, but nothing from later. The questionnaire scores are calculated as before:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("scores ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" questionnaire ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("stress_4     =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok(" stress_4,");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("stress       =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rowMeans");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("pick");#NormalTok("(stress_1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#NormalTok("stress_6), ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("burnout      =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rowMeans");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("pick");#NormalTok("(burnout_1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#NormalTok("burnout_6), ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("support      =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rowMeans");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("pick");#NormalTok("(support_1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#NormalTok("support_6), ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("satisfaction =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rowMeans");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("pick");#NormalTok("(satisfaction_1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#NormalTok("satisfaction_4), ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("  ) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(student_id, stress, burnout, support, satisfaction)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("dropout_data ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" students ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(scores, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(semesters ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(semester ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok("semester), ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("considering_dropout =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout, ");#AttributeTok("levels =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok("))) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok("student_id, ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok("supervisor_id, ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok("workshop, ");#SpecialCharTok("-");#NormalTok("workshop_sessions)");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("dim");#NormalTok("(dropout_data)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 600  21");],));
]
]
Two details matter. The outcome must be a factor, and its first level is treated as the event to predict, so the levels are set to put #NormalTok("\"Yes\""); first. And the workshop variables are removed: the workshop took place after the first semester, so it would not be known when the prediction is made. Using information that would not be available at the time of prediction is a form of #strong[data leakage], and it makes a model look better than it can ever be in practice.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("dropout_data ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("count");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("share =");#NormalTok(" n ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("sum");#NormalTok("(n))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("  considering_dropout   n share");],
[#NormalTok("1                 Yes  90  0.15");],
[#NormalTok("2                  No 510  0.85");],));
]
]
Only about 15% of students answer "Yes". Such an #strong[imbalanced] outcome is common in real prediction problems, from rare diseases to fraud, and, as the evaluation below shows, it catches out anyone who judges a model by accuracy alone.

== Splitting the data
<splitting-the-data>
The most important rule of machine learning follows from the simulation above: #strong[never judge a model on the data it learned from.] The first step is therefore to split the data. The #strong[training set] is used to build and tune the model. The #strong[test set] is locked away and used only once, at the very end, to estimate how the final model will perform on new students. The function #NormalTok("initial_split()"); does the split, and #NormalTok("strata"); makes sure that both sets have the same share of "Yes" answers:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2026");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("dropout_split ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("initial_split");#NormalTok("(dropout_data, ");#AttributeTok("prop =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.75");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("strata =");#NormalTok(" considering_dropout)");],
[#NormalTok("dropout_train ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("training");#NormalTok("(dropout_split)");],
[#NormalTok("dropout_test  ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("testing");#NormalTok("(dropout_split)");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("nrow");#NormalTok("(dropout_train)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 449");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("nrow");#NormalTok("(dropout_test)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 151");],));
]
]
Three quarters of the students are used for training and a quarter are set aside for the final test.

== Preparing the data
<preparing-the-data>
Most models need the data prepared first: missing values filled in, categories turned into numbers, and numeric predictors put on a common scale. A #strong[recipe] lists these steps:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("dropout_recipe ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("recipe");#NormalTok("(considering_dropout ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ., ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" dropout_train) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("step_impute_median");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("all_numeric_predictors");#NormalTok("()) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("step_dummy");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("all_nominal_predictors");#NormalTok("()) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("step_normalize");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("all_numeric_predictors");#NormalTok("())");],));
]
The first line says that #NormalTok("considering_dropout"); is the outcome and every other column (#NormalTok(".");) is a predictor. The step #NormalTok("step_impute_median()"); then replaces each missing numeric value with the median of that variable. The step #NormalTok("step_dummy()"); turns each categorical predictor into #strong[dummy variables]: 0/1 columns, one per category except a reference category, as #NormalTok("lm()"); did automatically in Chapter 8. Finally, #NormalTok("step_normalize()"); turns every numeric predictor into z-scores, so they are on the same scale; some models, such as k-nearest neighbours below, depend on distances and need this.

The recipe only describes the steps. To see what it produces, #NormalTok("prep()"); estimates what it needs from the training data (the medians, the means and standard deviations), and #NormalTok("bake()"); applies it:

