[#NormalTok("ℹ The deprecated feature was likely used in the factoextra package.");],
[#NormalTok("  Please report the issue at <https://github.com/kassambara/factoextra/issues>.");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: Using `size` aesthetic for lines was deprecated in ggplot2 3.4.0.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ Please use `linewidth` instead.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ The deprecated feature was likely used in the ggpubr package.");],
[#NormalTok("  Please report the issue at <https://github.com/kassambara/ggpubr/issues>.");],));
]
#figure([
#box(image("14-advanced-clustering_files/figure-typst/fig-bic-1.svg", alt: "Line chart of BIC against number of clusters from 1 to 8, with one line per model type. BIC rises steeply from 1 to 3 clusters and is highest at 3, then levels off or falls."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
BIC for Gaussian mixture models with one to eight clusters. Each line is one covariance structure; higher is better.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-bic>


BIC chooses 3 clusters with the EVE structure (ellipsoidal clusters of equal size and orientation, but different shapes). The best four-cluster model is about 8.7 BIC points behind. A common rule of thumb reads a BIC difference above 6 as strong evidence and above 10 as very strong, so the data favours three profiles, although four remain a reasonable alternative (exercise 1 explores them).

=== Describing the profiles
<describing-the-profiles>
As in Chapter 9, a cluster means something only once it is described:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("profiles ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" profiles ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("mutate");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("gmm_cluster =");#NormalTok(" profile_gmm");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("classification)");],
[],
[#NormalTok("profiles ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("summarise");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("students =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("n");#NormalTok("(), ");#FunctionTok("across");#NormalTok("(sleep_hours");#SpecialCharTok(":");#NormalTok("satisfaction, ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(.x), ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")),");],
[#NormalTok("            ");#AttributeTok(".by =");#NormalTok(" gmm_cluster) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("arrange");#NormalTok("(gmm_cluster)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("  gmm_cluster students sleep_hours study_hours caffeine_mg exercise_days stress");],
[#NormalTok("1           1      231         6.7        17.9       151.7           2.2    3.3");],
[#NormalTok("2           2      174         7.2        25.0       126.2           3.7    2.6");],
[#NormalTok("3           3      194         5.5        41.7       284.1           1.3    3.6");],
[#NormalTok("  support satisfaction");],
[#NormalTok("1     2.8          2.7");],
[#NormalTok("2     3.6          3.9");],
[#NormalTok("3     3.3          3.1");],));
]
]
The three profiles are clear. One is #strong[balanced]: the most sleep and exercise, the least stress, and the highest support and satisfaction. One is #strong[overloaded]: long study weeks, short sleep, a lot of caffeine, little exercise, and the most stress. The third, the largest, combines few study hours with low support and low satisfaction: students who seem #strong[disengaged or isolated]. The cluster numbers are arbitrary labels. A cross-table compares the solution with k-means:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("table");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("gmm =");#NormalTok(" profile_gmm");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("classification, ");#AttributeTok("kmeans =");#NormalTok(" kmeans_clusters");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("cluster)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("   kmeans");],
[#NormalTok("gmm   1   2   3   4");],
[#NormalTok("  1   5 191  34   1");],
[#NormalTok("  2   0   3 166   5");],
[#NormalTok("  3  80   3   0 111");],));
]
]
The mixture model's three profiles correspond closely to Chapter 9's four k-means clusters: the balanced and disengaged groups largely match, and the two k-means "overloaded" clusters, one of them with very high caffeine, are joined into one. The mixture model, with its more flexible cluster shapes, did not need to split the overloaded students in two.

=== Certainty of assignment
<certainty-of-assignment>
The matrix #NormalTok("profile_gmm$z"); holds each student's membership probabilities, one column per cluster, and #NormalTok("profile_gmm$uncertainty"); is 1 minus the largest of them.

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("head");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("round");#NormalTok("(profile_gmm");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("z, ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("  [,1] [,2] [,3]");],
[#NormalTok("1 0.63 0.37    0");],
[#NormalTok("2 0.99 0.00    0");],
[#NormalTok("3 0.14 0.86    0");],
[#NormalTok("4 0.00 0.00    1");],
[#NormalTok("5 0.10 0.90    0");],
[#NormalTok("6 1.00 0.00    0");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("certainty ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("apply");#NormalTok("(profile_gmm");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("z, ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", max)");],
[#FunctionTok("sum");#NormalTok("(certainty ");#SpecialCharTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.8");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 79");],));
]
]
The call #NormalTok("apply(..., 1, max)"); takes the maximum of each row. Most students belong clearly to one profile, but 79 have less than an 80% probability for their most likely profile. #ref(<fig-uncertainty>, supplement: [Figure]) shows where they are.

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("fviz_mclust");#NormalTok("(profile_gmm, ");#AttributeTok("what =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"uncertainty\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: The `<scale>` argument of `guides()` cannot be `FALSE`. Use \"none\" instead as");],
[#NormalTok("of ggplot2 3.3.4.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ The deprecated feature was likely used in the factoextra package.");],
