#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: Removed 1 row containing missing values or values outside the scale range");],
[#NormalTok("(`geom_point()`).");],));
]
#figure([
#box(image("16-time-series_files/figure-typst/fig-lag1-1.svg", alt: "Scatter plot of this week's visits against last week's visits. The points form a clear rising band from bottom left to top right."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Weekly visits against the visits of the previous week. Busy weeks tend to follow busy weeks, and quiet weeks quiet weeks.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-lag1>


The points rise from left to right: knowing last week's visits says a good deal about this week's. The #strong[autocorrelation] at lag $k$ puts a number on this relationship: it is the correlation between the series and itself $k$ steps earlier, between each week and the week before (lag 1), two weeks before (lag 2), and so on. The function #NormalTok("ACF()"); calculates it:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("visits_acf ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" visits ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("ACF");#NormalTok("(visits, ");#AttributeTok("lag_max =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("60");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("visits_acf ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("slice");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("26");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("52");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tsibble: 4 x 2 [1W]");],
[#NormalTok("       lag    acf");],
[#NormalTok("  <cf_lag>  <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1       1W  0.690");],
[#NormalTok("2       2W  0.572");],
[#NormalTok("3      26W -0.244");],
[#NormalTok("4      52W  0.684");],));
]
]
The autocorrelation is 0.69 at lag 1, as the scatter plot suggested, and 0.68 at lag 52: a week tends to be like the same week a year earlier. At lag 26 it is negative: half a year apart, a busy term week is often matched with a quiet summer week. #ref(<fig-acf>, supplement: [Figure]) shows all the lags. Strong autocorrelation at the seasonal lag is the signature of seasonality; if a series had no autocorrelation at all, there would be nothing to forecast beyond its average.

#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("autoplot");#NormalTok("(visits_acf) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("12");#NormalTok(")");],));
