== Decomposition
<decomposition>
#strong[Decomposition] recovers the parts of a pattern from an observed series: a smooth #strong[trend], a repeating #strong[seasonal] component, and the #strong[remainder], what is left over, which corresponds to the noise of #ref(<fig-parts>, supplement: [Figure]). #strong[STL] (seasonal and trend decomposition using loess) is a flexible, widely used method. #NormalTok("season(period = 52)"); tells it the length of the seasonal pattern, and #NormalTok("robust = TRUE"); stops unusual weeks from distorting the trend and season:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("visits_stl ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" visits ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("model");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("STL");#NormalTok("(visits ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("season");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("period =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("52");#NormalTok("), ");#AttributeTok("robust =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("components");#NormalTok("()");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("autoplot");#NormalTok("(visits_stl) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("11");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: `autoplot.dcmp_ts()` was deprecated in fabletools 0.6.0.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ Please use `ggtime::autoplot.dcmp_ts()` instead.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ Graphics functions have been moved to the {ggtime} package. Please use");],
[#NormalTok("  `library(ggtime)` instead.");],));
]
#figure([
#box(image("16-time-series_files/figure-typst/fig-stl-1.svg", alt: "Four stacked panels sharing a time axis. Top: the original series. Second: the trend, rising slowly and levelling off in the last year. Third: the seasonal component, the same shape each year with exam peaks and summer lows. Bottom: the remainder, mostly small, with one large positive spike in autumn 2023."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
STL decomposition of weekly visits into trend, seasonal pattern, and remainder.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-stl>


In #ref(<fig-stl>, supplement: [Figure]) the series is the sum of the three components below it, just as in the artificial example. The trend rises from about 28 to about 38 visits a week, levelling off in the last year; the seasonal component repeats the academic year; and the remainder is mostly small noise. The largest remainder is the unusual week:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("visits_stl ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("as_tibble");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("slice_max");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("abs");#NormalTok("(remainder), ");#AttributeTok("n =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(week, visits, trend, season_52, remainder)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 3 × 5");],
[#NormalTok("      week visits trend season_52 remainder");],
[#NormalTok("    <week>  <int> <dbl>     <dbl>     <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 2023 W43     71  35.2      8.08      27.7");],
[#NormalTok("2 2024 W48     32  38.4     12.1      -18.5");],
[#NormalTok("3 2024 W20     51  37.1     31.3      -17.5");],));
]
]
In the week of 23 October 2023, there were 71 visits, about 28 more than trend and season would predict. When asked, the doctors remember at once: it was the university's mental health awareness week, with posters everywhere encouraging students to seek help. The remainder is where such events show up. It is a real week, not an error, so it is kept, and the robust decomposition makes sure it does not distort the forecasts.

Notice also that the seasonal swings grow slightly as the trend rises: the peaks get higher and the summer lows stay low. When the seasonal pattern grows in proportion to the level of the series, it is usual to model the #strong[logarithm] of the series, on which the swings become constant. fable handles this automatically: write #NormalTok("log(visits)"); in the model, and the forecasts are transformed back to visits.

== Forecasting
<forecasting>
=== Simple benchmarks
<simple-benchmarks>
Every forecasting method should be compared with simple #strong[benchmark] methods, just as every predictive model in Chapters 11 to 13 was compared with a baseline. The three most common are the #strong[mean] method, which forecasts the average of all past observations; the #strong[naive] method, which forecasts the last observed value; and the #strong[seasonal naive] method, which forecasts the value from the same season in the last cycle (the same day last week, or the same week last year). For the clinic example, with its weekly cycle of 7 days, they give:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("clinic_fc ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" clinic ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("model");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("mean   =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("MEAN");#NormalTok("(visits),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("naive  =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("NAIVE");#NormalTok("(visits),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("snaive =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("SNAIVE");#NormalTok("(visits ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lag");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("  ) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("forecast");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("h =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("clinic_fc ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("as_tibble");#NormalTok("() ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(.model, day, .mean) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  tidyr");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("pivot_wider");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("names_from =");#NormalTok(" .model, ");#AttributeTok("values_from =");#NormalTok(" .mean)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 7 × 4");],
[#NormalTok("    day  mean naive snaive");],
[#NormalTok("  <dbl> <dbl> <dbl>  <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1    15  8.21     2     14");],
[#NormalTok("2    16  8.21     2     11");],
[#NormalTok("3    17  8.21     2     10");],
[#NormalTok("4    18  8.21     2     12");],
[#NormalTok("5    19  8.21     2      8");],
[#NormalTok("6    20  8.21     2      4");],
[#NormalTok("7    21  8.21     2      2");],));
]
]
The function #NormalTok("model()"); fits several models at once, each named; #NormalTok("forecast(h = 7)"); forecasts 7 steps ahead; and #NormalTok(".mean"); holds the point forecasts. The mean method forecasts 8.2 visits every day; the naive method repeats the last value, a quiet Sunday, for the whole week; and the seasonal naive method repeats last week's pattern, which for this series is clearly the most sensible.

=== Two families of models
<two-families-of-models>
Two families of models go beyond the benchmarks. #strong[Exponential smoothing (ETS)] forecasts with weighted averages of past observations, with weights that decrease the further back the observations are, so that recent weeks count most. ETS models can track a changing level, a trend, and a seasonal pattern, each estimated from the data; the name stands for the three components it models, error, trend, and season. #strong[ARIMA] models describe how each observation depends on earlier observations and on earlier random shocks, and use the autocorrelation of the series to forecast it.

The functions #NormalTok("ETS()"); and #NormalTok("ARIMA()"); in fable choose the details of each model automatically, by a criterion like the BIC of Chapter 14. Both work best with short seasonal periods, such as 4 quarters, 7 days, or 12 months. A 52-week season is long, so two strategies are common for weekly data. The first is to #strong[decompose first]: remove the seasonal pattern with STL, forecast the #strong[seasonally adjusted] series (trend and remainder) with ETS, and add the seasonal pattern back, all of which #NormalTok("decomposition_model()"); does in one step. The second uses #strong[Fourier terms]: the seasonal pattern is described with a few smooth waves (sines and cosines) of different lengths, used as predictors in an ARIMA model; #NormalTok("fourier(period = 52, K = 6)"); uses six pairs of waves.

=== Training and test periods
<training-and-test-periods>
As in machine learning, forecasts must be judged on data the model has not seen. For time series, the test data must come #emph[after] the training data, because a forecast can only use the past. The models are trained on the first four academic years and tested on the fifth:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("visits_train ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" visits ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(week_start ");#SpecialCharTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("as.Date");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"2024-09-01\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("visits_test  ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" visits ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(week_start ");#SpecialCharTok(">=");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("as.Date");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"2024-09-01\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[],
[#NormalTok("visits_fit ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" visits_train ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("model");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("mean     =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("MEAN");#NormalTok("(visits),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("naive    =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("NAIVE");#NormalTok("(visits),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("snaive   =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("SNAIVE");#NormalTok("(visits ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("lag");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("52");#NormalTok(")),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("stl_ets  =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("decomposition_model");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("                 ");#FunctionTok("STL");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("log");#NormalTok("(visits) ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("season");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("period =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("52");#NormalTok("), ");#AttributeTok("robust =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok("),");],
[#NormalTok("                 ");#FunctionTok("ETS");#NormalTok("(season_adjust ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("season");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"N\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("               ),");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("fourier  =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("ARIMA");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("log");#NormalTok("(visits) ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("fourier");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("period =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("52");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("K =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("6");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("PDQ");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("  )");],));
]
In the STL model, #NormalTok("ETS(season_adjust ~ season(\"N\"))"); forecasts the seasonally adjusted series with no seasonal component of its own ("N" for none), because the seasonal pattern is added back from the decomposition. In the Fourier model, #NormalTok("PDQ(0, 0, 0)"); tells ARIMA not to model the season itself, because the Fourier terms do that.

Each model forecasts the 52 weeks of the test year, and #NormalTok("accuracy()"); compares the forecasts with what actually happened, using the RMSE and MAE of Chapter 13:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("visits_fc ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" visits_fit ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("forecast");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("h =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("52");#NormalTok(")");],
[],
[#NormalTok("visits_fc ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("accuracy");#NormalTok("(visits) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("select");#NormalTok("(.model, RMSE, MAE) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("arrange");#NormalTok("(RMSE)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("# A tibble: 5 × 3");],
[#NormalTok("  .model   RMSE   MAE");],
[#NormalTok("  <chr>   <dbl> <dbl>");],
[#NormalTok("1 stl_ets  7.40  5.57");],
[#NormalTok("2 snaive   9.16  6.96");],
[#NormalTok("3 fourier 10.3   7.81");],
[#NormalTok("4 mean    18.7  16.3 ");],
[#NormalTok("5 naive   27.5  23.5 ");],));
]
]
The STL and ETS model is best: its forecasts are off by 5.6 visits a week on average (MAE), against 7.0 for the seasonal naive benchmark, which simply repeats the previous year. The mean and naive methods, which ignore the seasonal pattern, are far worse. The Fourier model does worse than the seasonal naive benchmark: the counselling service's pattern has sharp steps (the summer drop happens from one week to the next), and smooth waves struggle to follow them. #ref(<fig-test-forecasts>, supplement: [Figure]) compares the two best models with the actual visits.

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("visits_fc ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(.model ");#SpecialCharTok("%in%");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"stl_ets\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"snaive\"");#NormalTok(")) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("autoplot");#NormalTok("(visits_test, ");#AttributeTok("level =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("NULL");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("labs");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("NULL");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Visits per week\"");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("colour =");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Model\"");#NormalTok(") ");#SpecialCharTok("+");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("theme_minimal");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("base_size =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("12");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: `autoplot.fbl_ts()` was deprecated in fabletools 0.6.0.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ Please use `ggtime::autoplot.fbl_ts()` instead.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ Graphics functions have been moved to the {ggtime} package. Please use");],
[#NormalTok("  `library(ggtime)` instead.");],));
]
#figure([
#box(image("16-time-series_files/figure-typst/fig-test-forecasts-1.svg", alt: "Line chart of weekly visits in the test year. The black line of actual visits shows the usual exam peaks and summer low. The STL and ETS forecast follows the actual line closely for most of the year. The seasonal naive forecast, a copy of the previous year, is more jagged, and in October 2024 it shows a spike that did not happen."))
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Forecasts for the test year (September 2024 to August 2025) from the STL and ETS model and the seasonal naive benchmark, with the actual visits in black.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-test-forecasts>


The seasonal naive forecast copies last year's noise along with its pattern: it even repeats the awareness week of October 2023 as a spike in October 2024. The STL and ETS model smooths the noise away and adds the trend, so its forecasts are both smoother and closer.

=== Forecasting next year
<forecasting-next-year>
With the model chosen, it is refitted on all five years, so the forecasts use the most recent information, and the next 52 weeks are forecast:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("final_fit ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" visits ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("model");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("stl_ets =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("decomposition_model");#NormalTok("(");],
