Several formats can be listed at once, and one render then produces all of them. Many journals and universities provide Quarto templates, installed as #strong[extensions], that format a document to their requirements. A whole thesis can be written as a #strong[Quarto book] (#NormalTok("project: type: book");), with one #NormalTok(".qmd"); file per chapter, exactly like this book.

=== Parameterised reports
<parameterised-reports>
The dean of each faculty would like a one-page summary for their own faculty. Rather than five reports, one report with a #strong[parameter] serves them all:

#Skylighting(([#CommentTok("---");],
[#AnnotationTok("title:");#CommentTok(" \"Wellbeing report\"");],
[#AnnotationTok("format:");#CommentTok(" html");],
[#AnnotationTok("params:");],
[#CommentTok("  faculty: \"Education\"");],
[#CommentTok("---");],
[],
[#InformationTok("```{r}");],
[#NormalTok("faculty_semesters ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" semesters ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(students, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(faculty ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" params");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("faculty)");],
[#InformationTok("```");],
[],
[#NormalTok("This report describes the ");#InformationTok("`r n_distinct(faculty_semesters$student_id)`");#NormalTok(" students");],
[#NormalTok("of the Faculty of ");#InformationTok("`r params$faculty`");#NormalTok(".");],));
The value #NormalTok("params$faculty"); is used in the code like any other value. Rendering from the Terminal with a different value produces each faculty's version:

#Skylighting(([#ExtensionTok("quarto");#NormalTok(" render faculty-report.qmd ");#AttributeTok("-P");#NormalTok(" faculty:");#StringTok("\"Health Sciences\"");],));
== Keeping the same package versions
<keeping-the-same-package-versions>
Packages change. A function's default may change between versions, or a function may be removed, so an analysis that runs today may give different results, or fail, in two years. #strong[renv] records the exact version of every package a project uses, and can restore them later on any computer. It has three main commands, run in the Console:

#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("renv");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("init");#NormalTok("()      ");#CommentTok("# once: give the project its own package library");],
[#NormalTok("renv");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("snapshot");#NormalTok("()  ");#CommentTok("# after installing or updating packages: record the versions");],
[#NormalTok("renv");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("restore");#NormalTok("()   ");#CommentTok("# on another computer, or later: install the recorded versions");],));
The first command, #NormalTok("renv::init()");, creates a file called #NormalTok("renv.lock");, which lists every package and its version, and gives the project its own private library of packages, so updating a package for one project does not affect the others. Share #NormalTok("renv.lock"); with your code, and anyone can rebuild your exact set of packages.

Even without renv, #strong[report the versions] of R and the key packages in your methods section. R can tell you:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("R.version.string");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] \"R version 4.4.3 (2025-02-28 ucrt)\"");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("packageVersion");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"lme4\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] '1.1.37'");],));
]
]
The function #NormalTok("sessionInfo()"); lists everything that is loaded, for a full record.

== Interactive dashboards with Shiny
<interactive-dashboards-with-shiny>
A report answers the questions its author thought of. Sometimes readers want to ask their own: "what about my faculty?", "what about sleep instead of wellbeing?". #strong[Shiny] turns R code into an interactive web application, without any knowledge of web programming.

A Shiny app rests on three ideas. #strong[Inputs] are the controls the reader uses, such as drop-down menus, buttons, and sliders. #strong[Outputs] are what the app shows in response, such as plots and tables. Every app therefore has two parts: the #strong[user interface] (#NormalTok("ui");), which describes what the user sees, the inputs and the places for the outputs, and the #strong[server] function, which contains the R code that produces the outputs from the inputs. The third idea is what makes Shiny work: #strong[reactivity]. Whenever an input changes, Shiny works out which outputs depend on it and reruns only their code. #ref(<fig-reactivity>, supplement: [Figure]) shows the connections in a wellbeing dashboard: choosing a different faculty updates both the plot and the table, while choosing a different measure updates them without refiltering the data.

#figure([
#block[
#box(image("17-reproducible-research_files\\figure-typst\\mermaid-figure-2.png", height: 2.52in, width: 5.77in))

]
], caption: figure.caption(
position: bottom, 
[
Reactivity in the wellbeing dashboard. When an input changes, everything downstream of it is updated.
]), 
kind: "quarto-float-fig", 
supplement: "Figure", 
)
<fig-reactivity>


=== A wellbeing dashboard
<a-wellbeing-dashboard>
The complete app, saved as a file called #NormalTok("app.R");, is:

