To share an app with people who do not use R, it must run on a server: #strong[shinyapps.io] offers free hosting for small apps, and many universities run #strong[Posit Connect]. #strong[Shinylive] can even turn a simple app into a web page that runs entirely in the reader's browser, with no server, using the same webR technology as this book's playground. Like any published result, a dashboard must protect participants: this one shows only averages, never individual students.

== Open science
<open-science>
Reproducibility inside your own project is the first step. #strong[Open science] goes further: making research checkable and reusable by others.

=== Sharing data and code
<sharing-data-and-code>
Share the code that produced every result, and the data if you can. Repositories such as #strong[OSF] (the Open Science Framework) and #strong[Zenodo] store them permanently and give them a #strong[DOI], a permanent identifier that can be cited in the thesis. A good package includes the raw data (or instructions for obtaining it), the cleaning and analysis code, the Quarto source of the report, the #NormalTok("renv.lock"); file, and a #strong[codebook] describing every variable, like the specification of the wellbeing dataset. A #strong[licence] tells others what they may do with it: CC BY for data and text, and the MIT licence for code, are common choices.

=== Protecting participants
<protecting-participants>
Research data about people can only be shared if the people cannot be identified. Removing names and student numbers is not enough: combinations of ordinary variables can identify someone. In the wellbeing data, a few combinations of faculty, study mode, gender, and having children describe only a handful of students:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("small_cells ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" students ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("count");#NormalTok("(faculty, study_mode, gender, has_children) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("arrange");#NormalTok("(n)");],
[#FunctionTok("head");#NormalTok("(small_cells, ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("          faculty study_mode gender has_children n");],
[#NormalTok("1       Education  Part-time   Male          Yes 3");],
[#NormalTok("2 Health Sciences  Part-time   Male          Yes 3");],
[#NormalTok("3      Humanities  Part-time Female          Yes 3");],
[#NormalTok("4       Education  Part-time Female          Yes 4");],));
]
]
Only 3 students are, for example, part-time male students with children in the Faculty of Education; in a small department, such a description may point to recognisable people. Before sharing, such #strong[small cells] are protected, for example by grouping categories, removing variables that are not needed, or sharing only summary data. Ethics approval and the consent form set what may be shared: if the consent form told students that only anonymised data would be shared, that is all that may be shared. When the real data cannot be shared at all, a #strong[synthetic] dataset with the same structure, like the one in this book, lets others run the code.

=== Preregistration
<preregistration>
Every analysis involves choices that are reasonable either way: whether to exclude outliers, whether to transform a skewed variable, which test to use, which control variables to include. Chapter 5 warned that when these choices are made after seeing the data, they can be steered, often unconsciously, towards a significant result. A simulation shows how much this matters. The code below creates data with no real difference between two groups, analyses it in five defensible ways, and records whether the first analysis, and whether #emph[any] of the five, gives a p-value below 0.05:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("forking_paths ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#ControlFlowTok("function");#NormalTok("() {");],
[#NormalTok("  group     ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rep");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok("), ");#AttributeTok("each =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("  score     ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rexp");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("60");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("rate =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("/");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(")       ");#CommentTok("# a skewed score, no real group difference");],
[#NormalTok("  covariate ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rnorm");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("60");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("  z ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("abs");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("as.numeric");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("scale");#NormalTok("(score)))");],
[#NormalTok("  p ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("t_test         =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(score ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" group)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("p.value,");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("no_outliers    =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(score[z ");#SpecialCharTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("] ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" group[z ");#SpecialCharTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("])");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("p.value,");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("log_scale      =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("log");#NormalTok("(score) ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" group)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("p.value,");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("rank_test      =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("wilcox.test");#NormalTok("(score ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" group)");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("p.value,");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("with_covariate =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("summary");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(score ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" group ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" covariate))");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("coefficients[");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok("]");],
[#NormalTok("  )");],
[#NormalTok("  ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("first_analysis =");#NormalTok(" p[[");#StringTok("\"t_test\"");#NormalTok("]] ");#SpecialCharTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.05");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("any_analysis =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("any");#NormalTok("(p ");#SpecialCharTok("<");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("0.05");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("}");],
[],
[#FunctionTok("set.seed");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("17");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("false_positives ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("rowMeans");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("replicate");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("2000");#NormalTok(", ");#FunctionTok("forking_paths");#NormalTok("()))");],
[#NormalTok("false_positives");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("first_analysis   any_analysis ");],
[#NormalTok("        0.0545         0.1085 ");],));
]
]
A single planned analysis gives a false positive in about 5% of the simulated studies, as intended. Choosing afterwards among five reasonable analyses raises the rate to about 11%, although there is nothing to find. No individual choice is wrong; the problem is choosing after seeing the result. This is sometimes called the #strong[garden of forking paths], and preregistration is the way out of it.

#strong[Preregistration] means recording the hypotheses, the design, and the planned analysis before seeing the data, in a time-stamped public registry such as OSF Registries or AsPredicted. It separates #strong[confirmatory] analyses, planned in advance, from #strong[exploratory] ones, found along the way, and protects against the temptation, conscious or not, to try analyses until one gives a significant result (Chapter 7). Exploratory findings are still welcome, but they are reported as such. In a #strong[registered report], a journal reviews and accepts the plan before the data is collected, so publication does not depend on the results.

=== Citing software
<citing-software>
R and its packages are the work of researchers who depend on being cited. The function #NormalTok("citation()"); gives the recommended citation for R itself, and #NormalTok("citation(\"lme4\")"); for a package:

