== Challenges every researcher meets
<challenges-every-researcher-meets>
Real projects rarely go as planned, and some problems are almost universal. Every real dataset is #strong[messy] and needs cleaning, which takes longer than the analysis; it is done in code, checked after every step, and never applied to the raw file itself (Chapter 3). #strong[Missing data] raises the question of #emph[why] it is missing before anything is done about it, because dropping incomplete cases can bias results when the missing data is not random, as with the students who left the programme (Chapter 6). #strong[Small samples] give wide confidence intervals and low power (Chapter 7) and make complex models overfit (Chapter 13); the remedies are to report effect sizes with intervals, keep models simple, and plan the sample size before collecting data with a power analysis (Chapter 5).

Analysis brings its own problems. #strong[Many tests] produce many false alarms, so the main analyses are decided in advance, multiple comparisons are corrected where needed, and exploratory results are reported as exploratory (Chapters 7, 8, and 17). #strong[Assumptions] are checked with plots rather than with tests alone, with robust tests, non-parametric tests, transformations, and mixed models as the alternatives (Chapters 7, 8, and 10). #strong[Correlation is not causation]: observational data rarely proves causes, so confounders must be thought through, as sleep was behind the caffeine effect, and randomised designs used where possible, as in the workshop study (Chapters 5 and 8). #strong[Prediction and explanation] are different aims: a model that predicts well may explain little, and the reverse (Chapter 11). Finally, results must be #strong[communicated] to supervisors, co-authors, and examiners who may use other software; a clear results chapter with tables, figures, and an appendix of code serves them all, and data can be exported with #NormalTok("write_csv()"); or #NormalTok("haven::write_sav()"); when they need it (Chapter 2).

