All of it is free. R must be installed first, because RStudio and Positron are programs #emph[for] R: they need R to run your code. Install R, then the editor.

== Installing R
<installing-r>
R is downloaded from #strong[CRAN], the Comprehensive R Archive Network, at #link("https://cran.r-project.org")[cran.r-project.org]. Always download the latest release. This book was built with R 4.4.3; any later version works.

=== Windows
<windows>
+ On the CRAN page, choose #emph[Download R for Windows], then #emph[base], then the link to download the latest version (a file such as #NormalTok("R-4.x.y-win.exe");).
+ Run the downloaded file. If Windows asks whether to allow the app to make changes, choose #emph[Yes]. If you have no administrator rights on a university computer, the installer offers to install for your user only, which works just as well.
+ Accept the default settings on every screen. The defaults install R in #NormalTok("C:\\Program Files\\R\\"); and register it so that RStudio finds it automatically.

=== macOS
<macos>
+ On the CRAN page, choose #emph[Download R for macOS].
+ Choose the right package for your Mac's processor. Newer Macs (from late 2020) have #strong[Apple silicon] (M1, M2, and later): use the file marked #emph[arm64]. Older Macs have #strong[Intel] processors: use the file marked #emph[x86\_64]. The Apple menu, #emph[About This Mac], shows which you have.
+ Open the downloaded #NormalTok(".pkg"); file and follow the installer, accepting the defaults.

=== Linux
<linux>
R is available in the package manager of every major Linux distribution, but the version there is often out of date. CRAN's #emph[Download R for Linux] page gives up-to-date instructions for Ubuntu, Debian, Fedora, and others. On Ubuntu, for example, the instructions add CRAN's repository and then install R with #NormalTok("sudo apt install r-base r-base-dev");. Follow the page for your distribution, because the exact commands change with each release.

=== Checking the installation
<checking-the-installation>
Open R (from the Start menu on Windows or the Applications folder on macOS) and type:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("R.version.string");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] \"R version 4.4.3 (2025-02-28 ucrt)\"");],));
]
]
If it prints a version number, R is working. You will rarely open R on its own again; from now on you will work in RStudio.

== Installing RStudio
<installing-rstudio>
+ Go to #link("https://posit.co/download/rstudio-desktop/")[posit.co/download/rstudio-desktop]. The page detects your operating system; step 1 on the page (installing R) is already done.
+ Download #strong[RStudio Desktop] (the free version) and install it like any other program, accepting the defaults. On macOS, drag RStudio into the Applications folder.
+ Open RStudio. It finds R automatically and shows the R version in the Console pane, in the lower left (Chapter 1 takes you on a tour of the panes).

RStudio includes #strong[Quarto], so the reports of Chapter 17 work without installing anything else. For PDF output, Quarto also needs LaTeX; install a small version once by typing #NormalTok("quarto install tinytex"); in RStudio's #strong[Terminal] tab.

=== Positron, an alternative
<positron-an-alternative>
#strong[Positron] (#link("https://positron.posit.co")[positron.posit.co]) is a newer editor from Posit, the company behind RStudio, built for both R and Python. Everything in this book works in Positron as well. RStudio is used in the book's instructions because it is still the most widely used, and most tutorials and university courses assume it.

=== No installation at all
<no-installation-at-all>
If you cannot install software, for example on a locked-down computer, two options need only a web browser:

- The book's #link("../playground/index.html")[playground] runs R in your browser, with exercises for every chapter.
- #strong[Posit Cloud] (#link("https://posit.cloud")[posit.cloud]) offers RStudio in the browser, with a free plan that is limited but enough to follow most chapters.

== Recommended RStudio settings
<recommended-rstudio-settings>
A few settings make RStudio work the way this book recommends. Open #emph[Tools \> Global Options]:

- #strong[General \> Basic]: untick #emph[Restore .RData into workspace at startup], and set #emph[Save workspace to .RData on exit] to #strong[Never]. Every session then starts clean, and your scripts, not a hidden saved workspace, hold your work. This is the single most important setting for reproducible work (Chapter 17).
- #strong[Code \> Editing]: tick #emph[Use native pipe operator, |\>], so that the pipe shortcut (Ctrl+Shift+M, or Cmd+Shift+M on a Mac) inserts the pipe used in this book (Chapter 3).
- #strong[Code \> Display]: tick #emph[Show margin] and #emph[Highlight R function calls], which make code easier to read.
- #strong[Appearance]: choose a font size and theme that are comfortable for you; dark themes are popular for long sessions.

