Two entries are collections rather than single packages. #strong[tidyverse] installs and loads dplyr, tidyr, stringr, readr, ggplot2, purrr, and a few others; #strong[tidymodels] does the same for the modelling packages of Chapters 11 to 15 (rsample, recipes, parsnip, workflows, tune, and yardstick, among others). Several model packages (ranger, kknn, kernlab, glmnet, xgboost, nnet) are rarely loaded by name: tidymodels calls them as #strong[engines], as in #NormalTok("set_engine(\"ranger\")");, but they must be installed.

== Finding and choosing packages
<finding-and-choosing-packages>
CRAN, R's official package archive, holds more than 20,000 packages, and many more are shared on GitHub. A few ways to find the right one:

- #strong[CRAN Task Views] (#link("https://cran.r-project.org/web/views/")[cran.r-project.org/web/views]) are curated lists of packages by topic, such as psychometrics, survival analysis, or time series, maintained by experts in each field.
- A package's #strong[vignettes], long-form tutorials that come with it, are the best introduction: #NormalTok("browseVignettes(\"dplyr\")"); lists them. Many packages also have a website with examples.
- Methods papers in journals such as the #emph[Journal of Statistical Software] and #emph[The R Journal] describe many packages in depth.

Before relying on a package for your thesis, a few signs show whether it is trustworthy: it is on CRAN (which checks that packages install and run); it was updated in the last year or two; it has documentation and examples; it is described in a published paper or widely used in your field; and its authors are known in the area. The packages in this book meet all or most of these.

== Citing packages
<citing-packages>
Package authors are researchers too, and citing their work is how they receive credit. #NormalTok("citation()"); gives the recommended reference for R itself, and #NormalTok("citation(\"package\")"); for a package:

#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("citation");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"psych\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("To cite package 'psych' in publications use:");],
[],
[#NormalTok("  William Revelle (2026). _psych: Procedures for Psychological,");],
[#NormalTok("  Psychometric, and Personality Research_. Northwestern University,");],
[#NormalTok("  Evanston, Illinois. R package version 2.6.4,");],
[#NormalTok("  <https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=psych>.");],
[],
[#NormalTok("A BibTeX entry for LaTeX users is");],
[],
[#NormalTok("  @Manual{,");],
[#NormalTok("    title = {psych: Procedures for Psychological, Psychometric, and Personality Research},");],
[#NormalTok("    author = {{William Revelle}},");],
[#NormalTok("    organization = {Northwestern University},");],
[#NormalTok("    address = {Evanston, Illinois},");],
[#NormalTok("    year = {2026},");],
[#NormalTok("    note = {R package version 2.6.4},");],
[#NormalTok("    url = {https://CRAN.R-project.org/package=psych},");],
[#NormalTok("  }");],));
]
]
Cite the packages that did substantial work in your analysis, and give their version numbers (Chapter 17). #NormalTok("packageVersion(\"psych\")"); shows the version installed on your computer.

= Appendix D: Common Errors and How to Fix Them
<sec-app-errors>
Everyone who writes R code sees error messages every day, experts included. An error is not a sign that you are doing badly; it is R telling you, as precisely as it can, what it could not do. This appendix collects the messages that readers of this book are most likely to meet, what each one means, and how to fix it. The messages below are real: each example was run when the book was built, so the wording is what your R will show (it can differ slightly between versions).

== Reading a message
<reading-a-message>
R produces three kinds of messages:

- An #strong[error] stops the code: nothing after it runs. It starts with #NormalTok("Error");.
- A #strong[warning] lets the code finish but tells you something may be wrong. It starts with #NormalTok("Warning");. Never ignore a warning without understanding it: several below mean your results contain missing values or were calculated differently from what you intended.
- A #strong[message] is information only, such as a package telling you it has loaded.

When a long message appears, read it from the end: the last lines usually say what went wrong, and lines starting with #NormalTok("ℹ"); point to where. Newer packages (dplyr, ggplot2, tidymodels) write especially helpful messages, often with a suggestion (#NormalTok("Did you mean ...?");).

== Starting out
<starting-out>
=== object '…' not found
<object-not-found>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(sleep_hours)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in h(simpleError(msg, call)): error in evaluating the argument 'x' in selecting a method for function 'mean': object 'sleep_hours' not found");],));
]
]
R does not know the name. The usual causes: a typing mistake (names are case-sensitive: #NormalTok("Sleep_hours"); is not #NormalTok("sleep_hours");); the object was never created, because the line that creates it was not run; or the name is a column inside a data frame, which must be reached through the data frame, as in #NormalTok("mean(semesters$sleep_hours)"); or inside a dplyr verb. If a document fails to render with this error although the code works in the Console, the object was created in the Console but not in the document (Chapter 17).

=== could not find function "…"
<could-not-find-function>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("Mean");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("5.5");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in Mean(c(6.5, 7, 5.5)): could not find function \"Mean\"");],));
]
]
Either the function name is misspelled (here, #NormalTok("mean"); with a capital M), or it belongs to a package that is not loaded. Load the package with #NormalTok("library()");, or write #NormalTok("package::function()");. The pipe #NormalTok("%>%"); from older code gives the same error until dplyr or magrittr is loaded; the native pipe #NormalTok("|>"); needs no package.

=== there is no package called '…'
<there-is-no-package-called>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("library");#NormalTok("(ggplot)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in library(ggplot): there is no package called 'ggplot'");],));
]
]
The package is not installed, or its name is misspelled (the package is ggplot2). Install it once with #NormalTok("install.packages(\"ggplot2\")");, then load it with #NormalTok("library(ggplot2)");. Appendix A lists every package the book uses.

=== unexpected symbol, unexpected ')', unexpected string constant
<unexpected-symbol-unexpected-unexpected-string-constant>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(") ");#AttributeTok("na.rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in parse(text = input): <text>:1:16: unexpected symbol");],
[#NormalTok("1: mean(c(6.5, 7) na.rm");],
[#NormalTok("                   ^");],));
]
]
A #strong[syntax error]: R cannot read the line at all. The #NormalTok("^"); marks where R got lost; the mistake is usually just before it. Here a comma is missing before #NormalTok("na.rm");. Other common causes are an extra or missing bracket (#NormalTok("unexpected ')'");), a missing comma between two pieces of text (#NormalTok("unexpected string constant");), and a missing quotation mark. RStudio highlights matching brackets and marks syntax errors with a red cross in the margin before you even run the code.

=== The Console shows #NormalTok("+"); and nothing happens
<the-console-shows-and-nothing-happens>
R is waiting for the rest of an unfinished command, usually because a bracket or quotation mark is not closed. Press #strong[Esc] to cancel, fix the line, and run it again.

=== argument "…" is missing, with no default
<argument-is-missing-with-no-default>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("rnorm");#NormalTok("()");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in rnorm(): argument \"n\" is missing, with no default");],));
]
]
A required argument was not given. The help page (#NormalTok("?rnorm");) lists the arguments; those without a default value (here #NormalTok("n");, the number of values) must be supplied.

=== A misspelled argument, with no error at all
<a-misspelled-argument-with-no-error-at-all>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#ConstantTok("NA");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok("), ");#AttributeTok("na_rm =");#NormalTok(" ");#ConstantTok("TRUE");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] NA");],));
]
]
Not every mistake produces a message. The argument is #NormalTok("na.rm");, not #NormalTok("na_rm");, and because #NormalTok("mean()"); accepts extra arguments, the misspelled one is silently ignored: the missing value is not removed, and the answer is #NormalTok("NA");. When a result looks wrong, check the spelling of every argument against the help page.

== Files and folders
<files-and-folders>
=== cannot open file …: No such file or directory
<cannot-open-file-no-such-file-or-directory>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("read.csv");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"studnets.csv\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in file(file, \"rt\"): cannot open file 'studnets.csv': No such file or");],
[#NormalTok("directory");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in file(file, \"rt\"): cannot open the connection");],));
]
]
R looked for the file in the #strong[working directory] and did not find it. Check the spelling of the file name (here #NormalTok("studnets");), check that the file is in the project folder, and build paths with #NormalTok("here::here()"); inside an RStudio Project (Chapter 1), so they work on any computer. #NormalTok("list.files()"); shows the files R can see.

=== cannot change working directory
<cannot-change-working-directory>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("setwd");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"C:/Users/Elaf/Documents/thesis\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in setwd(\"C:/Users/Elaf/Documents/thesis\"): cannot change working directory");],));
]
]
The folder does not exist on this computer, which is exactly why #NormalTok("setwd()"); with a full path breaks as soon as a script is shared or moved. Use an RStudio Project and relative paths instead (Chapter 1).

== Data
<data>
=== non-numeric argument to binary operator
<non-numeric-argument-to-binary-operator>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#StringTok("\"7\"");#NormalTok(" ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in \"7\" + 1: non-numeric argument to binary operator");],));
]
]
Arithmetic on text. The quotation marks make #NormalTok("\"7\""); a piece of text, not a number. In real data, this happens when a numeric column was imported as text, often because of one stray value such as #NormalTok("\"7 hrs\""); or a missing-value code. Check with #NormalTok("str()"); or #NormalTok("glimpse()");, and convert with #NormalTok("as.numeric()"); or #NormalTok("readr::parse_number()"); (Chapters 2 and 3).

=== argument is not numeric or logical: returning NA
<argument-is-not-numeric-or-logical-returning-na>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"6.5\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"7\"");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in mean.default(c(\"6.5\", \"7\")): argument is not numeric or logical:");],
[#NormalTok("returning NA");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] NA");],));
]
]
A #strong[warning], not an error, and the result is #NormalTok("NA");: the same problem as above, a numeric column stored as text. Convert the column first.

=== NAs introduced by coercion
<nas-introduced-by-coercion>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("as.numeric");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"6.5\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"7 hrs\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"six\"");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: NAs introduced by coercion");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] 6.5  NA  NA");],));
]
]
Values that could not be converted to numbers became #NormalTok("NA");. Look at which ones (#NormalTok("\"7 hrs\""); and #NormalTok("\"six\""); here) before going on: #NormalTok("parse_number()"); can rescue #NormalTok("\"7 hrs\"");, but #NormalTok("\"six\""); needs a decision (Chapter 3). Losing data silently through this warning is one of the most common problems in real analyses.

=== The result is NA
<the-result-is-na>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("mean");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#ConstantTok("NA");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] NA");],));
]
]
Not an error: any calculation that includes a missing value gives #NormalTok("NA");, because the true answer is unknown. Add #NormalTok("na.rm = TRUE"); to calculate from the available values, and report how many were missing (Chapter 6).

=== object '…' not found, inside dplyr
<object-not-found-inside-dplyr>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("students ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(facultty ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Education\"");#NormalTok(")");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in `filter()`:");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ In argument: `facultty == \"Education\"`.");],
[#NormalTok("Caused by error:");],
[#NormalTok("! object 'facultty' not found");],));
]
]
A column name is misspelled. dplyr says in which argument the problem is (#NormalTok("ℹ In argument: ...");) and which name it could not find. #NormalTok("names(students)"); lists the correct names.

=== arguments imply differing number of rows; replacement has … rows
<arguments-imply-differing-number-of-rows-replacement-has-rows>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("student =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("sleep =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in data.frame(student = 1:3, sleep = c(6.5, 7)): arguments imply differing number of rows: 3, 2");],));
]
]
Every column of a data frame must have the same length. The data behind a new column has more or fewer values than the data frame has rows; check the lengths with #NormalTok("length()"); and #NormalTok("nrow()");.

=== \$ operator is invalid for atomic vectors
<operator-is-invalid-for-atomic-vectors>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("x ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("sleep =");#NormalTok(" ");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("study =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("30");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("x");#SpecialCharTok("$");#NormalTok("sleep");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in x$sleep: $ operator is invalid for atomic vectors");],));
]
]
#NormalTok("$"); works on data frames and lists, but #NormalTok("x"); is a vector. Use #NormalTok("x[\"sleep\"]"); for a vector, and check what kind of object you have with #NormalTok("class()"); or #NormalTok("str()");.

=== subscript out of bounds
<subscript-out-of-bounds>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("results ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("list");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("7");#NormalTok(")");],
[#NormalTok("results[[");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok("]]");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in results[[3]]: subscript out of bounds");],));
]
]
You asked for an element that does not exist: the third element of a list with two. Check the length with #NormalTok("length()");, and the names with #NormalTok("names()");.

=== invalid factor level, NA generated
<invalid-factor-level-na-generated>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("answer ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("factor");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"Yes\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"No\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("answer[");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("] ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Maybe\"");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in `[<-.factor`(`*tmp*`, 1, value = \"Maybe\"): invalid factor level, NA");],
[#NormalTok("generated");],));
]
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("answer");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("[1] <NA> No  ");],
[#NormalTok("Levels: No Yes");],));
]
]
A factor accepts only its existing levels (Chapter 2); any other value becomes #NormalTok("NA");. Add the level first with #NormalTok("levels()");, or work with the column as text and convert it to a factor at the end.

=== Detected an unexpected many-to-many relationship
<detected-an-unexpected-many-to-many-relationship>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("students_small ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("id =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("), ");#AttributeTok("group =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#StringTok("\"A\"");#NormalTok(", ");#StringTok("\"B\"");#NormalTok("))");],
[#NormalTok("scores_small ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("id =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok("), ");#AttributeTok("score =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("3.2");#NormalTok(", ");#FloatTok("4.1");#NormalTok("))");],
[#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(students_small, scores_small, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(id))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in left_join(students_small, scores_small, join_by(id)): Detected an unexpected many-to-many relationship between `x` and `y`.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ Row 1 of `x` matches multiple rows in `y`.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ Row 1 of `y` matches multiple rows in `x`.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ If a many-to-many relationship is expected, set `relationship =");],
[#NormalTok("  \"many-to-many\"` to silence this warning.");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("  id group score");],
[#NormalTok("1  1     A   3.2");],
[#NormalTok("2  1     A   4.1");],
[#NormalTok("3  1     B   3.2");],
[#NormalTok("4  1     B   4.1");],));
]
]
A #strong[join] (Chapter 3) found identifiers that appear more than once in #emph[both] tables, so every copy was matched with every other, multiplying rows. Usually one of the tables should have had one row per identifier: check for duplicates with #NormalTok("count(id) |> filter(n > 1)");, and remove them or join by more columns.

=== Can't combine …  and … 
<cant-combine-and>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("tidyr");#SpecialCharTok("::");#FunctionTok("pivot_longer");#NormalTok("(students, ");#AttributeTok("cols =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(age, gender))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in `tidyr::pivot_longer()`:");],
[#NormalTok("! Can't combine `age` <integer> and `gender` <character>.");],));
]
]
#NormalTok("pivot_longer()"); puts the chosen columns into one column, so they must be of the same type: here a number and a text column. Reshape only columns of the same type, or convert them first.

== Plots
<plots>
=== Cannot use #NormalTok("+"); with a single argument
<cannot-use-with-a-single-argument>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("plot ");#OtherTok("<-");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(students, ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" age))");],
[#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("geom_histogram");#NormalTok("()");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error:");],
[#NormalTok("! Cannot use `+` with a single argument.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ Did you accidentally put `+` on a new line?");],));
]
]
In ggplot2, the #NormalTok("+"); must come at the #emph[end] of a line, not the start of the next one. Otherwise R thinks the first line is complete, and the second line starts with a stray #NormalTok("+");. The message even asks: "Did you accidentally put #NormalTok("+"); on a new line?"

=== #NormalTok("mapping"); must be created by #NormalTok("aes()"); … Did you use #NormalTok("%>%"); or #NormalTok("|>"); instead of #NormalTok("+");?
<mapping-must-be-created-by-aes-did-you-use-or-instead-of>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(students, ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" age)) ");#SpecialCharTok("|>");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("geom_histogram");#NormalTok("()");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in `geom_histogram()`:");],
[#NormalTok("! `mapping` must be created by `aes()`.");],
[#NormalTok("✖ You've supplied a <ggplot2::ggplot> object.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ Did you use `%>%` or `|>` instead of `+`?");],));
]
]
Layers of a ggplot are added with #NormalTok("+");, not with the pipe. The pipe passes data #emph[into] #NormalTok("ggplot()");\; after that, it is #NormalTok("+"); all the way.

=== object '…' not found, in a plot
<object-not-found-in-a-plot>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(students) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("geom_point");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" age, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" financial_worry)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error: object 'age' not found");],));
]
]
Columns must be mapped inside #NormalTok("aes()");: #NormalTok("geom_point(aes(x = age, y = financial_worry))");. Outside #NormalTok("aes()");, R looks for objects called #NormalTok("age"); and #NormalTok("financial_worry"); and does not find them (Chapter 4 on mapping versus setting).

=== #NormalTok("stat_count()"); must only have an x or y aesthetic
<stat_count-must-only-have-an-x-or-y-aesthetic>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("ggplot");#NormalTok("(students, ");#FunctionTok("aes");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("x =");#NormalTok(" faculty, ");#AttributeTok("y =");#NormalTok(" age)) ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("geom_bar");#NormalTok("()");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in `geom_bar()`:");],
[#NormalTok("! Problem while computing stat.");],
[#NormalTok("ℹ Error occurred in the 1st layer.");],
[#NormalTok("Caused by error in `setup_params()`:");],
[#NormalTok("! `stat_count()` must only have an x or y aesthetic.");],));
]
]
#NormalTok("geom_bar()"); counts the rows in each category, so it takes only #NormalTok("x");. To plot a value you have calculated, such as a mean per faculty, use #NormalTok("geom_col()");.

== Statistical tests and models
<statistical-tests-and-models>
=== grouping factor must have exactly 2 levels
<grouping-factor-must-have-exactly-2-levels>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(age ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" faculty, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" students)");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in t.test.formula(age ~ faculty, data = students): grouping factor must have exactly 2 levels");],));
]
]
A two-sample t-test compares exactly two groups, but #NormalTok("faculty"); has five. Use ANOVA for more than two groups (Chapter 8), or filter the data to the two groups you want to compare.

=== not enough 'x' observations
<not-enough-x-observations>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("t.test");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#FloatTok("6.5");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in t.test.default(c(6.5)): not enough 'x' observations");],));
]
]
The test needs more data than it was given, often because filtering or missing values left too few cases. Check the number of cases with #NormalTok("nrow()"); or #NormalTok("sum(!is.na(x))");.

=== contrasts can be applied only to factors with 2 or more levels
<contrasts-can-be-applied-only-to-factors-with-2-or-more-levels>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("lm");#NormalTok("(age ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" gender, ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("filter");#NormalTok("(students, gender ");#SpecialCharTok("==");#NormalTok(" ");#StringTok("\"Female\"");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Error in `contrasts<-`(`*tmp*`, value = contr.funs[1 + isOF[nn]]): contrasts can be applied only to factors with 2 or more levels");],));
]
]
A categorical predictor has only one value in the data used, here because the data was filtered to women only, so there is nothing to compare. Remove the predictor, or check the filtering.

=== Chi-squared approximation may be incorrect
<chi-squared-approximation-may-be-incorrect>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("chisq.test");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("matrix");#NormalTok("(");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("3");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("4");#NormalTok("), ");#AttributeTok("nrow =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("2");#NormalTok("))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning in chisq.test(matrix(c(3, 1, 2, 4), nrow = 2)): Chi-squared");],
[#NormalTok("approximation may be incorrect");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([],
[#NormalTok("    Pearson's Chi-squared test with Yates' continuity correction");],
[],
[#NormalTok("data:  matrix(c(3, 1, 2, 4), nrow = 2)");],
[#NormalTok("X-squared = 0.41667, df = 1, p-value = 0.5186");],));
]
]
Some expected counts are below 5, so the chi-square test's p-value may be inaccurate (Chapter 7). Use Fisher's exact test (#NormalTok("fisher.test()");), or combine small categories.

=== glm.fit: fitted probabilities numerically 0 or 1 occurred; algorithm did not converge
<glm.fit-fitted-probabilities-numerically-0-or-1-occurred-algorithm-did-not-converge>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("glm");#NormalTok("(passed ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" hours, ");#AttributeTok("family =");#NormalTok(" binomial,");],
[#NormalTok("    ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("data.frame");#NormalTok("(");#AttributeTok("hours =");#NormalTok(" ");#DecValTok("1");#SpecialCharTok(":");#DecValTok("10");#NormalTok(", ");#AttributeTok("passed =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("c");#NormalTok("(");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("0");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(", ");#DecValTok("1");#NormalTok(")))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: glm.fit: algorithm did not converge");],));
]
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("Warning: glm.fit: fitted probabilities numerically 0 or 1 occurred");],));
]
]
In a logistic regression, a predictor separates the outcomes perfectly (here, everyone above 5 hours passed), so the coefficients become enormous and meaningless, and the fitting algorithm may also report that it did not converge. With real data, this usually means a very small group or a predictor that is almost the outcome itself. Check the cross-table of the outcome and the predictor, and consider simplifying the model.

=== boundary (singular) fit: see help('isSingular')
<boundary-singular-fit-see-helpissingular>
#block[
#Skylighting(([#FunctionTok("lmer");#NormalTok("(wellbeing ");#SpecialCharTok("~");#NormalTok(" semester ");#SpecialCharTok("+");#NormalTok(" (semester ");#SpecialCharTok("|");#NormalTok(" supervisor_id),");],
[#NormalTok("     ");#AttributeTok("data =");#NormalTok(" ");#FunctionTok("left_join");#NormalTok("(semesters, students, ");#FunctionTok("join_by");#NormalTok("(student_id)))");],));
#block[
#Skylighting(([#NormalTok("boundary (singular) fit: see help('isSingular')");],));
]
]
A mixed-effects model estimated some random-effect variance as zero, or a correlation as exactly ±1: the random effects part is too complex for the data (Chapter 10). Simplify it, for example by removing the random slope: #NormalTok("(1 | supervisor_id)");.

=== Observations deleted due to missingness
<observations-deleted-due-to-missingness>
Not an error, but a line in the output of #NormalTok("summary()"); for #NormalTok("lm()"); and #NormalTok("glm()");: rows with a missing value in any variable of the model are left out. Check how many with #NormalTok("nobs(model)");, report the number used, and think about whether the missing rows differ from the others (Chapters 6 and 8).

